<div id="offline" class="offline" hidden></div>
<!-- The PHP running this page against php2shell (1.10.218): said to an
     administrator while it may be affected. -->
<div id="phpsafety" class="offline" hidden></div>
<!-- A change that could cut the server off, waiting to be kept (1.10.228):
     a new network, or a change of the firewall. -->
<div id="trials" class="trials" hidden></div>

<!-- What the directory says has changed since somebody remembered it. On
     every page rather than on the Domain page: an account appearing in the
     group that runs the domain is not news that waits until somebody opens
     the right tab. It goes when the watching is switched off or somebody
     remembers what the directory says now; "Take it off the page" only
     hides it for whoever pressed it, until the next change. -->
<div id="dir-alarm" class="dirband" hidden>
  <div class="what" id="dir-alarm-says"></div>
  <div class="btnrow">
    <button class="btn small" type="button" id="dir-alarm-open">What changed</button>
    <button class="btn small" type="button" id="dir-alarm-hide"
      title="Takes this band off the page for you, on this browser, until something else changes. It clears nothing: what was noticed stays noticed, stays red on the Domain page, and is still there for everybody else.">Take it off the page</button>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="tabs" role="tablist">
  <button role="tab" aria-selected="true"  data-tab="overview" data-icon="chart"
          title="The shape of the last day: how much is failing, who is trying hardest, which names they are trying and where they are coming from.">Overview</button>
  <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-tab="bans" data-icon="ban"
          title="Who may not reach the VPN right now, why, for how long, and whether the router is carrying it. The history of every ban there has ever been is under it.">Banned<span class="count"
          id="tabn-bans"></span></button>
  <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-tab="failed" data-icon="cancel"
          title="Every authentication the two ISE nodes refused. Click a row for the whole record, exactly as ISE wrote it.">Failed attempts<span class="count"
          id="tabn-failed"></span></button>
  <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-tab="logins" data-icon="person"
          title="Everybody who did get in, in two lists: the AnyConnect VPN logins, with the firewall each session was handed, and the TACACS logins to switches and routers.">Logins</button>
  <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-tab="traffic" data-icon="net" hidden
          title="What crossed the firewall, out of its own log: every session it let through or refused, what its web filter and its SSL inspection did, who signed in to its VPN and to the firewall itself. Its failed sign-ins earn a ban only while that source is switched on under Settings → Banning.">Traffic<span class="count"
          id="tabn-traffic"></span></button>
  <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-tab="windows" data-icon="shield" hidden
          title="What the Windows domain controllers saw: the accounts the domain locked and why, who it turned away and from which machine, what was changed in Active Directory, and the handful of patterns worth looking at. Its failed logons from the internet earn a ban only while that source is switched on under Settings → Banning.">Domain<span class="count"
          id="tabn-windows"></span></button>
  <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-tab="router" data-icon="router"
          title="What the Mikrotik is actually carrying, beside what is known here. The two halves rarely disagree, and this is where it shows when they do — and where a pair in high availability is held against itself.">Firewall</button>
  <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-tab="events" data-icon="list"
          title="Every ban, every change of a setting, every sign-in and every conversation with the router — and the daemon’s own log.">Events</button>
  <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-tab="logs" data-icon="log"
          title="Every log file this reads - ISE, NPS, TACACS+, eduroam, the token servers, the domain controllers: how far each has been read, when, and whether anything is wrong with it.">Logs</button>
  <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-tab="settings" data-icon="gear"
          title="Everything this installation is configured to do, the whitelist, the public lists, GeoIP, the router, the accounts and the database.">Settings</button>
</div>

<!-- ===================== Overview ===================== -->
<section class="panel" id="tab-overview">
  <div class="grid top">
    <div class="card metric crit" id="card-failed"><div class="label">Failed, last 24 h</div>
      <div class="value" id="m-failed">–</div>
      <div class="sub" id="m-failed-sub">&nbsp;</div></div>
    <div class="card metric ok" id="card-passed"><div class="label">Passed, last 24 h</div>
      <div class="value" id="m-passed">–</div>
      <div class="sub" id="m-passed-sub">&nbsp;</div></div>
    <div class="card metric warn" id="card-blocked"><div class="label">Kept out now</div>
      <div class="value" id="m-blocked">–</div>
      <div class="sub" id="m-blocked-sub">&nbsp;</div></div>
    <div class="card metric info" id="card-router"><div class="label">The firewall</div>
      <div class="value" id="m-router">–</div>
      <div class="sub" id="m-router-sub">&nbsp;</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="card full" style="margin-top:14px">
    <h2>Authentications
      <span class="right">
        <select id="ov-hours" title="How far back the chart and the cards under it look. A window on what is shown; it changes nothing about what is kept.">
          <option value="6">last 6 hours</option>
          <option value="24" selected>last 24 hours</option>
          <option value="72">last 3 days</option>
          <option value="168">last week</option>
          <option value="720">last month</option>
          <option value="8760">last year</option>
        </select>
      </span></h2>
    <p class="note">Everything being read, as one line of time. Each bar is split by the
      door it came through — the VPN at the bottom, then TACACS+, the network, and the
      token server and the visitors’ network above those. Where the domain controllers
      are being read, what they refused stands beside them in blue, on the same scale —
      a refusal is a refusal whoever wrote it down. What they LET THROUGH has a scale of
      its own on the right, because one person signing in once writes several records on
      a controller and on one ruler it would bury everything else. Only the VPN is ever
      judged. Press a bar to open that hour.</p>
    <div class="chart" id="ov-chart"><div class="empty">…</div><div class="tip"></div></div>
    <div class="legend">
      <span class="leg-ise" title="Refused VPN sign-ins: AnyConnect, and anything else ISE answers over RADIUS. This is what the banning is made of."><i style="background:#f85149"></i>failed &middot; VPN</span>
      <span class="leg-ise" title="Refused sign-ins to a switch or router over TACACS+. A different system and a different door: a burst here is somebody at the network kit, not at the VPN."><i style="background:#ffa657"></i>failed &middot; TACACS</span>
      <span class="leg-ise" title="Refused 802.1X and MAB on the LAN and on Wi-Fi: machines on sockets and radios. None of this is ever judged and none of it can earn anybody a ban — it is here so that a quiet VPN and a network full of endpoints failing to authenticate are not the same picture."><i style="background:#d2a8ff"></i>failed &middot; 802.1X / MAB</span>
      <span class="leg-ise" title="VPN sign-ins that succeeded, so that a burst of refusals can be read against whether anybody was actually getting in."><i style="background:#3fb950"></i>passed &middot; VPN</span>
      <span class="leg-ise" title="Sign-ins to a switch or router that succeeded. The commands run afterwards are not counted here: they belong to the sign-in, not beside it."><i style="background:#58d3f7"></i>passed &middot; TACACS</span>
      <span class="leg-ise" title="Machines let on to a socket or a radio by 802.1X or by MAB."><i style="background:#a371f7"></i>passed &middot; 802.1X / MAB</span>
      <span id="leg-tac-bad" hidden title="Sign-ins a tac_plus server of this site's own turned away, out of the daemon's own log. A different system from the TACACS+ that is ISE's, with its own colour. Never judged and never banned."><i style="background:#bf8700"></i>failed &middot; TAC_Plus</span>
      <span id="leg-tac-good" hidden title="Sittings at a switch prompt that a tac_plus server of this site's own let in. The commands run during them are not counted here: they belong to the sitting, not beside it."><i style="background:#1f6feb"></i>passed &middot; TAC_Plus</span>
      <span id="leg-nps-vpn-bad" hidden title="VPN sign-ins a Windows NPS server refused. A different system from ISE, with its own colour, and never judged: the banning weighs ISE's VPN and nothing else."><i style="background:#a40e26"></i>failed &middot; NPS VPN</span>
      <span id="leg-nps-net-bad" hidden title="802.1X and MAB a Windows NPS server refused: machines on sockets and radios. Never judged and never banned."><i style="background:#6e40c9"></i>failed &middot; NPS 802.1X / MAB</span>
      <span id="leg-nps-vpn-good" hidden title="VPN sign-ins a Windows NPS server let through."><i style="background:#196c2e"></i>passed &middot; NPS VPN</span>
      <span id="leg-nps-net-good" hidden title="Machines a Windows NPS server let on to a socket or a radio, by 802.1X or by MAB."><i style="background:#7ee787"></i>passed &middot; NPS 802.1X / MAB</span>
      <span id="leg-rad-vpn-bad" hidden title="VPN sign-ins a FreeRADIUS server of this site's own refused. A different system from ISE and NPS, with its own colour; counted for a ban only while The VPN through a FreeRADIUS of your own is on under Banning."><i style="background:#ff7b72"></i>failed &middot; FreeRADIUS VPN</span>
      <span id="leg-rad-net-bad" hidden title="802.1X and MAB a FreeRADIUS server of this site's own refused: machines on sockets and radios. Never judged and never banned."><i style="background:#bc8cff"></i>failed &middot; FreeRADIUS 802.1X / MAB</span>
      <span id="leg-rad-vpn-good" hidden title="VPN sign-ins a FreeRADIUS server of this site's own let through."><i style="background:#aff5b4"></i>passed &middot; FreeRADIUS VPN</span>
      <span id="leg-rad-net-good" hidden title="Machines a FreeRADIUS server of this site's own let on to a socket or a radio, by 802.1X or by MAB."><i style="background:#0e8a8a"></i>passed &middot; FreeRADIUS 802.1X / MAB</span>
      <span id="leg-otp-bad" hidden title="One-time passwords the token server refused: a wrong number, one that had been used already, or an account with nothing enrolled. Never judged and never banned — the address on such a record is the token server itself."><i style="background:#f778ba"></i>failed &middot; OTP</span>
      <span id="leg-otp-good" hidden title="One-time passwords the token server accepted. On a site that asks for a token this is the other half of a VPN sign-in, and it is in no ISE log anywhere."><i style="background:#e3b341"></i>passed &middot; OTP</span>
      <span id="leg-edu-bad" hidden title="Sign-ins the visitors' network refused — a RADIUS server of this site's own, not ISE at all. Never judged and never banned: the only address on one of those records is the wireless controller the request came through."><i style="background:#ff9f68"></i>failed &middot; eduroam</span>
      <span id="leg-edu-good" hidden title="Sign-ins the visitors' network let through. On a site inside eduroam this is most of what happens all day and none of it is a VPN sign-in — which is why it has a colour of its own rather than being counted in with them."><i style="background:#2dd4bf"></i>passed &middot; eduroam</span>
      <span id="leg-dom-bad" hidden title="What the Windows domain controllers refused: wrong passwords, Kerberos and NTLM turned away, and accounts locked out. Drawn against the same scale as the bars beside it — a refusal is a refusal whoever wrote it down — but as a bar of its own, because a sign-in checked against the directory is already counted at the door it came through and adding it in would count the same morning twice. Never judged and never a ban."><i style="background:#8b9dff"></i>refused &middot; the domain</span>
      <span id="leg-dom-good" hidden title="Sign-ins the domain controllers let through — AGAINST THE SCALE ON THE RIGHT, in the same blue as its numbers. Reading the directory, changes to it and everything else a controller writes down are not counted here; only people getting in. One person signing in once writes a Kerberos ticket, an NTLM validation and a logon, and every service they open writes another, so this runs to tens of thousands an hour where the doors run to hundreds. On one ruler with them it would be a wall of blue with the whole VPN an inch high underneath it."><i style="background:#79c0ff"></i>signed in &middot; the domain</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- The doors. Everything under this line belongs to whichever one is
       chosen: the VPN keeps the four cards it has always had, and every other
       door is built from what is counted behind it. -->
  <div class="doors" id="ov-doors" role="tablist"></div>
  <p class="note doorsays" id="ov-doorsays"></p>

  <!-- ...the VPN, which is what this page has always been about. -->
  <div id="ov-door-vpn">
  <div class="grid halves" style="margin-top:14px">
    <div class="card">
      <h2>Busiest addresses</h2>
      <p class="note">Addresses by how many failed <b>VPN</b> attempts they have made in the
        window above — the only ones that are ever judged. A switch refusing MAB all day is
        on the Failed attempts page under its own tab and is not counted here.
        Click one to see everything known about it, and to ban it or let it back in.
        <b>Kept out</b> says whether it is blocked right now, and a red bar down the
        left of a row says the same at a glance.</p>
      <div class="scroll"><table class="rows" id="ov-attackers"><thead><tr>
        <th class="addrcell">Address</th><th class="placecell"></th>
        <th class="cccell">Country</th><th class="num">Tries</th>
        <th class="num">Names</th>
        <th>Last</th><th class="mark">Kept out</th>
        </tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>
      <div class="center" id="ov-attackers-empty" hidden>Nobody has failed yet</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h2>Names being tried</h2>
      <p class="note">The names failing at the <b>VPN</b>, and from how many different addresses
        each. One name from forty addresses is a password spray; forty names from one address is
        somebody working through a list. A MAC that cannot get on to a socket is not a name and
        is not here.</p>
      <div class="scroll"><table class="plain" id="ov-users"><thead><tr>
        <th></th><th>Name</th><th class="num">Tries</th><th class="num">Addresses</th>
        <th>Last</th>
      </tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>
      <div class="center" id="ov-users-empty" hidden>Nothing yet</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid" style="margin-top:14px">
    <div class="card">
      <h2>Where they come from</h2>
      <p class="note">Only ever shown, never judged: a country is not something an address
        chooses, and a scanner in Rotterdam looks exactly like an employee in Rotterdam. The
        router's country list, under Settings, is the place to act on one.</p>
      <div id="ov-countries"></div>
      <div class="center" id="ov-countries-empty" hidden>The country database has not been
        fetched</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  </div><!-- /ov-door-vpn -->

  <!-- ...and every other door, drawn from what is counted behind it. -->
  <div id="ov-door-other" hidden></div>
</section>

<!-- ===================== Banned ===================== -->
<section class="panel" id="tab-bans" hidden>
  <div class="card full">
    <h2>Ask about one address</h2>
    <p class="note">Everything this machine knows about it: whether it is kept out and why, what
      it has tried, whether anybody has ever got in from it, which public lists name it and what
      country it is in.</p>
    <div class="btnrow">
      <input id="look-ip" type="text" placeholder="45.9.148.9, or 2a01:430::5"
             title="One address, IPv4 or IPv6. It does not have to be banned, or even known: whatever is on record for it is shown, and if there is nothing the answer says so."
             style="max-width:280px" autocomplete="off">
      <button class="btn" id="look-go">Ask about it</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card full" id="bans-card" style="margin-top:14px">
    <h2>Kept out right now
      <span class="right" id="bans-rule"></span></h2>
    <p class="note">Everything being kept out at this moment, and why each one is.</p>
    <div id="bans-note" class="btnhelp"></div>
    <div id="bans-list"></div>
    <div class="btnrow ban-only" style="margin-top:12px">
      <input id="ban-ip" type="text" placeholder="89.24.5.7, 89.24.5.0/24 or 2a01:430::/48"
             title="One address or one whole subnet, IPv4 or IPv6. A subnet is written as a prefix and covers everything inside it, which is what to use when somebody has rented a range and is working through it."
             style="min-width:270px" autocomplete="off">
      <select id="ban-for" title="How long the ban above lasts. &quot;For good&quot; means it stays until somebody takes it off; any of the others put a clock on it and the address comes back by itself when the clock runs out. This is one ban made by hand and does not touch the ladder in Settings.">
        <option value="10">for 10 minutes</option>
        <option value="60">for an hour</option>
        <option value="1440">for a day</option>
        <option value="10080">for a week</option>
        <option value="0" selected>for good</option>
      </select>
      <button class="btn primary ban-only" id="ban-add">Ban this one</button>
      <button class="btn admin-only" id="ban-fold">Fold into subnets…</button>
      <button class="btn danger admin-only" id="ban-clear">Let them all back in</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Who is counting up but has not reached the bar. Folded away, because on
       a quiet morning it is empty and on a busy one it is the longest list on
       the page - and because somebody who opens this tab is usually asking
       about a ban that already happened. -->
  <div class="card full" style="margin-top:14px">
    <h2>Counting up to a ban <span class="right" id="soon-count"></span></h2>
<p class="note">Nothing here is banned yet. These are the addresses the counting can see,
      and how far each one still has to go, and for which source. What is counted is the
      sources switched on under Settings → Banning → Where the bans come from: the VPN, and -
      where those are on - logins to network devices, at the firewall and at the domain from
      the internet. A socket or a radio (802.1X, MAB) is recorded and shown, never
      judged.</p>
    <details class="fold" id="soon-fold">
      <summary title="Every address the counting can see right now: inside the window, under the bar, and not banned. Each row says how many attempts it has made against how many it may make, and which of the two rules it will reach first. Folded away because on a quiet morning it is empty and on a busy one it is the longest list on this page.">Addresses on their way <span class="n" id="soon-n"></span></summary>
      <div class="body" id="soon-body"></div>
    </details>
    <div class="btnrow admin-only" style="margin-top:10px">
      <button class="btn cmd danger" id="soon-clear" data-icon="forget">Clear all</button>
    </div>
    <p class="btnhelp" id="soon-from" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="card full" data-hold="1" style="margin-top:14px">
    <h2>Ban history
      <span class="right" id="past-count"></span></h2>
    <p class="note">Kept long after an address is let back in: it is what the ladder counts, and
      what somebody reads when they want to know why an address they have never heard of was on
      the router last Tuesday.</p>
    <div class="filters">
      <input type="search" id="past-q" placeholder="an address, a reason, a name…"
             title="Searches the whole history on the server and not just the page in front of you: the address, the reason it was banned and who did it. Case does not matter.">
      <select id="past-country" title="Only bans of addresses in one country. The country is written down when the ban is made, so a history older than this is filled in from the country database as it stands now."><option value="">every country</option></select>
      <button class="btn small" id="past-clear">Clear</button>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll rowsonly"><table class="rows" id="past-table" data-server-sort="1"><thead><tr>
      <th></th><th>Started</th><th>Ended</th><th>Address</th><th>Why</th>
      <th>Source</th><th>How it ended</th>
      <th class="num">Turned away</th><th>By</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>
    <div class="center" id="past-empty" hidden>Nobody has ever been banned</div>
    <div class="pager" id="past-pager"></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===================== Failed attempts ===================== -->
<section class="panel" id="tab-failed" hidden>
  <div class="card full" data-hold="1">
    <h2>Failed attempts <span class="right" id="failed-count"></span></h2>
    <div class="subtabs" id="f-kinds" role="tablist"></div>
    <p class="note" id="f-note">Every attempt at the VPN the two ISE nodes refused, newest
      first. Click a row for the whole record.</p>
    <div class="filters">
      <input type="search" id="f-q" placeholder="an address, a name, a reason…"
             title="Searches every refused attempt on the server, not the page in front of you: the address, the user name offered, what ISE said, the tunnel group and the device. Case does not matter.">
      <select id="f-hours" title="How far back to look. This is a filter on what is shown and changes nothing about what is kept — records are kept for as long as Settings → The database says.">
        <option value="0">all of it</option>
        <option value="1">last hour</option>
        <option value="24" selected>last 24 hours</option>
        <option value="168">last week</option>
        <option value="720">last month</option>
        <option value="8760">last year</option>
        <option value="custom" hidden>custom</option>
        <option value="pick">choose dates…</option>
      </select>
      <select id="f-device" title="Only attempts that arrived through one network device, by the name ISE calls it. Which devices may earn a ban is a different setting, under The ISE logs."><option value="">every device</option></select>
      <select id="f-server" title="Only what one of the two ISE nodes wrote. Both are read all the time; this only decides what is shown."><option value="">both nodes</option></select>
      <select id="f-country" title="Only attempts from one country, as the country database puts them. Addresses the database has no country for are ZZ."><option value="">every country</option></select>
      <span class="picks" id="f-picks"></span>
      <select id="f-limit" title="How many rows are asked for at a time. A bigger page is fewer presses and a slower answer; the pager underneath walks through the rest either way.">
        <option value="50">50 a page</option>
        <option value="100" selected>100 a page</option>
        <option value="250">250 a page</option>
      </select>
      <button class="btn small" id="f-clear">Clear</button>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll rowsonly"><table class="rows" id="f-table" data-server-sort="1"><thead><tr id="f-head">
      </tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>
    <div class="center" id="f-empty" hidden>Nothing matches</div>
    <div class="pager" id="f-pager"></div>
    <!-- The domain's refusals are not rows of this table and never will be:
         they are a different system, a different set of columns, and a
         sign-in checked against the directory is already counted at the door
         it came through. They are HERE because this is the page somebody is
         on when the question occurs to them. -->

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===================== Logins ===================== -->
<section class="panel" id="tab-logins" hidden>
  <div class="card full" data-hold="1">
    <h2>Successful logins <span class="right" id="passed-count"></span></h2>
    <div class="subtabs" id="p-kinds" role="tablist"></div>
    <p class="note" id="p-note">Everybody who got on to the VPN, newest first. Click a
      row for the whole record.</p>
    <div class="filters">
      <input type="search" id="p-q" placeholder="a name, an address, a profile…"
             title="Searches every successful login on the server: the name, the address it came from, the authorisation profile and the device. Case does not matter.">
      <select id="p-hours" title="How far back to look. A filter on what is shown; it changes nothing about what is kept.">
        <option value="0">all of it</option>
        <option value="1">last hour</option>
        <option value="24" selected>last 24 hours</option>
        <option value="168">last week</option>
        <option value="720">last month</option>
        <option value="8760">last year</option>
        <option value="custom" hidden>custom</option>
        <option value="pick">choose dates…</option>
      </select>
      <select id="p-device" title="Only logins through one device — for AnyConnect that is the VPN headend, for TACACS the switch or router somebody signed in to."><option value="">every device</option></select>
      <select id="p-server" title="Only what one of the two ISE nodes wrote. Both are read all the time; this only decides what is shown."><option value="">both nodes</option></select>
      <select id="p-country" title="Only logins from one country, as the country database puts them. Addresses the database has no country for are ZZ."><option value="">every country</option></select>
      <span class="picks" id="p-picks"></span>
      <select id="p-limit" title="How many rows are asked for at a time. A bigger page is fewer presses and a slower answer.">
        <option value="50">50 a page</option>
        <option value="100" selected>100 a page</option>
        <option value="250">250 a page</option>
      </select>
      <button class="btn small" id="p-clear">Clear</button>
    </div>
    <div class="btnrow" id="acls-bar" hidden>
      <button class="btn small" id="acls-open" data-icon="wall">The firewalls handed
        down<span class="n" id="acls-count"></span></button>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll rowsonly"><table class="rows" id="p-table" data-server-sort="1"><thead><tr id="p-head">
      </tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>
    <div class="center" id="p-empty" hidden>Nothing matches</div>
    <div class="pager" id="p-pager"></div>
  </div>

</section>

<!-- ===================== Router ===================== -->
<section class="panel" id="tab-router" hidden>
  <div class="card full">
    <h2>What the firewall is carrying <span class="right" id="router-when"></span></h2>
    <p class="note">What each firewall is actually carrying, read from it, beside what
      this machine says it should be.</p>
    <div class="btnrow">
      <button class="btn" id="router-read">Read it now</button>
      <button class="btn primary ban-only" id="router-sync">Put the lists on it</button>
      <button class="btn" id="router-ios">Access list for a Cisco router…</button>
      <button class="btn danger admin-only" id="router-purge" title="Take every entry carrying this daemon’s mark off the router — the banned addresses, the country list, or both. Anything you put on those lists by hand stays, every other address list is never mentioned, and your firewall rules are not touched. The bans themselves stay here, so &quot;Put the lists on it&quot; afterwards puts them all back.">Take ours off it…</button>
      <span class="muted small" id="router-note" style="align-self:center"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="going wide" id="router-going" hidden>
      <div class="bar"><span></span></div>
      <div class="says"></div>
    </div>
    <div id="router-out" style="margin-top:14px"></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===================== Events ===================== -->
<!-- ===================== The domain ===================== -->
<!-- ===================== Traffic ===================== -->
<!-- What crossed the firewall, out of the firewall's own log. Read only and
     judged never: the banning weighs ISE's VPN and nothing else. Every card
     and the chart are counted ahead of time, so the page is as quick on
     three million sessions a day as on three hundred; the list underneath
     is the rows themselves. -->
<section class="panel" id="tab-traffic" hidden>
  <div class="subtabs pinned" id="t-pages" role="tablist">
    <button type="button" role="tab" data-page="over" class="on" aria-selected="true">
      <span>Overview</span></button>
    <button type="button" role="tab" data-page="list" aria-selected="false">
      <span>Sessions</span><span class="n" id="t-n-list"></span></button>
    <!-- The NAT rules' own lines and the DHCP leases (MikroTik, 1.10.206):
         only where a firewall writes any. -->
    <button type="button" role="tab" data-page="nat" aria-selected="false" hidden>
      <span>NAT</span><span class="n" id="t-n-nat"></span></button>
    <button type="button" role="tab" data-page="web" aria-selected="false">
      <span>Web</span><span class="n" id="t-n-web"></span></button>
    <button type="button" role="tab" data-page="threat" aria-selected="false" hidden>
      <span>Threats</span><span class="n" id="t-n-threat"></span></button>
    <button type="button" role="tab" data-page="cvpn" aria-selected="false">
      <span>Client VPN</span><span class="n" id="t-n-cvpn"></span></button>
    <button type="button" role="tab" data-page="s2s" aria-selected="false">
      <span>Site-to-site VPN</span><span class="n" id="t-n-s2s"></span></button>
    <!-- Not lines but the VPN as it stands: who is connected, which
         tunnels are up. Only where a firewall writes its VPN at all. -->
    <button type="button" role="tab" data-page="vpn" aria-selected="false" hidden>
      <span>Connected now</span><span class="n" id="t-n-vpn"></span></button>
    <button type="button" role="tab" data-page="admin" aria-selected="false">
      <span>Administrators</span><span class="n" id="t-n-admin"></span></button>
    <button type="button" role="tab" data-page="system" aria-selected="false">
      <span>The firewall</span><span class="n" id="t-n-system"></span></button>
    <button type="button" role="tab" data-page="dhcp" aria-selected="false" hidden>
      <span>DHCP</span><span class="n" id="t-n-dhcp"></span></button>
    <!-- A MikroTik read through its API or over ssh (1.10.211): the router
         as it is - its interfaces, addresses, routes, leases, VPN, rules.
         Only where one is read; without it this tab is the lines alone. -->
    <button type="button" role="tab" data-page="router" aria-selected="false" hidden>
      <span>Router</span><span class="n" id="t-n-router"></span></button>
    <!-- Lines that fit none of the pages above: only where there are any. -->
    <button type="button" role="tab" data-page="other" aria-selected="false" hidden>
      <span>Other</span><span class="n" id="t-n-other"></span></button>
    <!-- Every line at once: not a button of its own, the pages above are
         that, split. It is where a search from the top for an address or a
         person lands, and its button shows only while it is open. -->
    <button type="button" role="tab" data-page="all" aria-selected="false" hidden>
      <span>Every line</span><span class="n" id="t-n-all"></span></button>
    <!-- Everything the firewall said about itself at once: where the cards
         of the overview point for a failed sign-in or one of its own lines.
         Not a button of its own - the four pages above are that, split. -->
    <button type="button" role="tab" data-page="events" aria-selected="false" hidden>
      <span>What the firewall said</span><span class="n" id="t-n-events"></span></button>
    <!-- Which firewall the whole tab is about, where there is more than one. -->
    <span class="t-right">
    <span class="t-fws" id="t-fws" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Which firewall" hidden></span>
    <span class="t-window">
      <select id="t-window" title="How far back the cards, the chart and the lists look. This changes what is shown, never what is kept: Settings → The firewall log says how long that is.">
        <option value="1">last hour</option>
        <option value="6">last 6 hours</option>
        <option value="24" selected>last 24 hours</option>
        <option value="72">last 3 days</option>
        <option value="168">last week</option>
        <option value="custom" hidden>custom</option>
        <option value="pick">choose dates…</option>
      </select>
    </span>
    </span>
  </div>

  <div id="t-page-over">
    <div class="grid top" id="t-cards"></div>
    <div class="card full" style="margin-top:14px">
      <h2>Hour by hour <span class="right" id="t-chart-note"></span></h2>
      <p class="note">Every session the firewall wrote down, split by what it did with it.
        Let through above the line, refused below it, so a burst of somebody knocking
        stands out of a busy day rather than vanishing into it. Press a bar for the
        lines of that hour.</p>
      <div class="chart t-chart" id="t-chart"></div>
      <div class="legend" id="t-legend"></div>
      <div class="t-stored" id="t-stored"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="t-tops" id="t-tops"></div>
  </div>

  <div id="t-page-list" hidden>
    <div class="card full" data-hold="1">
      <h2 id="t-list-title">Sessions <span class="right" id="t-count"></span></h2>
      <p class="note" id="t-list-note">Every line of the firewall's log in the window
        above, newest first. Press a row for everything the line says.</p>
      <div class="t-search">
        <div class="t-qbox">
          <input type="text" id="t-q" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false"
                 placeholder="A word, or fields: srcip:10.1.2.3 -dstport:443 verdict:deny OR host:*.example.com"
                 aria-label="Search the firewall's lines">
          <button type="button" class="btn small primary" id="t-qgo" data-icon="search">Search</button>
          <button type="button" class="btn small" id="t-help" data-icon="info">How to search</button>
          <div class="t-suggest" id="t-suggest" hidden></div>
        </div>
        <div class="t-said" id="t-said"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="filters">
        <select id="t-verdict" title="Only what the firewall did with it.">
          <option value="">whatever it did</option>
          <option value="allow">let through</option>
          <option value="deny,block,fail">refused or blocked</option>
          <option value="deny">refused by a policy</option>
          <option value="block">blocked by the web filter</option>
          <option value="fail">failed sign-ins</option>
          <option value="info">said only</option>
        </select>
        <select id="t-kind" title="Only one kind of line.">
          <option value="">every kind of line</option>
          <option value="traffic">sessions, through it and to it</option>
          <option value="traffic/forward">through the firewall</option>
          <option value="traffic/local">to the firewall itself</option>
          <option value="utm">the web filter and SSL inspection</option>
          <option value="utm/webfilter">the web filter</option>
          <option value="utm/ssl">SSL inspection</option>
          <option value="utm/dns">the DNS filter</option>
          <option value="traffic/stray">packets of sessions already closed (FTD)</option>
          <option value="traffic/routing">no route (FTD)</option>
          <option value="traffic/inspect">protocol inspection (FTD)</option>
          <option value="utm/file">file policy (FTD)</option>
          <option value="utm/ips">intrusion policy (FTD)</option>
          <option value="utm/malware">malware (FTD)</option>
          <option value="utm/si">Security Intelligence (FTD)</option>
          <option value="utm/virus">antivirus</option>
          <option value="utm/app-ctrl">application control</option>
          <option value="event">what the firewall said</option>
          <option value="event/vpn">its VPN</option>
          <option value="event/ssl">TLS to the firewall itself (FTD)</option>
          <option value="event/ha">failover (FTD)</option>
          <option value="event/threat">threat detection (FTD)</option>
          <option value="traffic/nat">the NAT rules (MikroTik)</option>
          <option value="event/dhcp">DHCP leases (MikroTik)</option>
        </select>
        <span class="picks" id="t-picks"></span>
        <select id="t-limit" title="How many rows are asked for at a time.">
          <option value="50">50 a page</option>
          <option value="100" selected>100 a page</option>
          <option value="250">250 a page</option>
        </select>
        <button class="btn small" id="t-clear">Clear</button>
        <div class="t-quick" id="t-quick"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="scroll rowsonly t-scroll" id="t-scroll"><table class="rows t-rows" id="t-table" data-server-sort="1"><thead><tr id="t-head">
        </tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>
      <div class="center" id="t-empty" hidden>Nothing matches</div>
      <div class="pager" id="t-pager"></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div id="t-page-vpn" hidden>
    <div class="card full">
      <h2>On the remote-access VPN now <span class="right" id="tv-c-n"></span></h2>
      <p class="note">Who is connected, put together from the firewall's own lines: a
        session that began and has not ended. Press one for everything known about it.</p>
      <div class="t-vpn-tools">
        <input type="text" id="tv-q" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false"
               placeholder="a name, an address, a group…" aria-label="Narrow both lists">
        <span class="muted" id="tv-said"></span>
      </div>
      <div id="tv-clients"></div>
      <div class="pager" id="tv-c-pager"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card full" style="margin-top:14px">
      <h2>Site-to-site tunnels up <span class="right" id="tv-t-n"></span></h2>
      <p class="note">The IPsec tunnels up at the moment, out of their coming up, their
        rekeys and their SAs. Press one for its networks, its SAs and what crossed it.</p>
      <div id="tv-tunnels"></div>
      <div class="pager" id="tv-t-pager"></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div id="t-page-router" hidden>
    <div class="t-vpn-tools">
      <input type="text" id="tr-q" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false"
             placeholder="an interface, an address, a MAC, a name…"
             aria-label="Narrow every table of the router">
      <span class="muted" id="tr-said"></span>
    </div>
    <div id="tr-body"></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="tab-windows" hidden>
  <!-- Two pages rather than one very long one. The overview is what somebody
       opens this tab to see; the events themselves are where they go next,
       and going there should not mean scrolling past four cards every time.
       The strip stays at the top of the window while either of them is being
       read, because the way back is the thing people look for first. -->
  <div class="subtabs pinned" id="w-pages" role="tablist">
    <button type="button" role="tab" data-page="over" class="on" aria-selected="true">
      <span>Overview</span></button>
    <button type="button" role="tab" data-page="events" aria-selected="false">
      <span>The events themselves</span><span class="n" id="w-pages-n"></span></button>
  </div>
  <div id="w-page-over">
  <!-- No row of tiles. Four big numbers across the top push the cards that
       answer something below the fold, and they are the same four numbers on
       every page that has them. What was worth keeping out of them is the
       line under the window picker: the counts, each one still opening the
       events it stands for. -->
  <div class="card full">
    <h2>Worth looking at
      <span class="right">
        <select id="w-window" title="How far back these patterns are counted. A window on what is shown; it changes nothing about what is kept.">
          <option value="6">last 6 hours</option>
          <option value="24" selected>last 24 hours</option>
          <option value="72">last 3 days</option>
          <option value="168">last week</option>
          <option value="720">last month</option>
        </select>
      </span></h2>
    <p class="note">Patterns in what the domain wrote down, counted rather than guessed.
      None of it is judged and none of it bans anybody: it is a list of things to look at,
      and each one opens the events it is made of.</p>
    <!-- Every card on this page is a walk of a window of a table that on a
         real site holds millions, and there are twenty of them. What is
         already worked out comes back at once; the rest is set going behind
         and arrives on the next poll, a few seconds later. This says so,
         rather than leaving the page looking empty. -->
    <div class="note center" id="w-working" hidden>Still working the rest of this out —
      what is ready is below, and the rest arrives in a moment.</div>
    <div class="doorsettled" id="w-tally"></div>
    <!-- Why no controller is marked anywhere, on an installation where none
         is. Drawn only when the directory has named none of its own. -->
    <div id="w-nodc" hidden></div>
    <div id="w-incidents"></div>
    <div class="center" id="w-incidents-none" hidden>Nothing in this window stands out.</div>
    <!-- How far along it is. The job runs in the daemon and says where it
         has got to there, so this is drawn from the answer rather than
         remembered in the browser: it is the same bar after a reload, on
         another tab, and on somebody else's screen.

         The button that starts it is not here: this page is for reading, and
         a page for reading should not carry the one button that spends ten
         minutes rewriting what is on it. It lives with the settings it obeys,
         under Settings - The domain controllers. What stays here is the bar,
         because somebody who pressed it there comes back HERE to watch, and
         it runs the width of the page so that what it is doing fits on one
         line under it. -->
    <div class="going wide" id="w-going" hidden>
      <div class="bar"><span id="w-going-fill"></span></div>
      <div class="says" id="w-going-says"></div>
    </div>
    <!-- ...and what the directory itself said, which is half of how the
         patterns above are weighed. Drawn here rather than on a page of its
         own because it is not a thing to read: it is the answer to "is that
         actually happening", and the place that question is asked is under
         the list it changes. -->
    <div id="w-directory" class="dirsays"></div>
  </div>

  <div class="card full" id="w-locks-card" style="margin-top:14px">
    <h2>Accounts the domain locked <span class="right small" id="w-locks-n"></span></h2>
    <p class="note">The lock itself says only that it happened. What everybody actually asks
      — which machine, which program, which password — is in the refusals in the half hour
      before it, so they are here too, and so is who unlocked it. Press one for the whole story.</p>
    <div class="scroll"><table class="rows" id="w-locks"><thead><tr>
      <th>When</th><th>Account</th><th>From</th><th class="throughcell">Through</th>
      <th class="num">Refusals before it</th><th>How it ended</th><th>Controller</th>
    </tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>
    <div class="center" id="w-locks-none" hidden>Nothing has been locked in this window</div>
  </div>

  <div id="w-views"></div>
  </div>

  <div id="w-page-events" hidden>
  <div class="card full" data-hold="1">
    <h2>The events themselves <span class="right" id="w-events-n"></span></h2>
    <div class="subtabs" id="w-kinds" role="tablist"></div>
    <p class="note" id="w-note">Everything the domain controllers wrote down, newest first.
      Press a row for the event exactly as Windows wrote it — every field of it, what else
      happened to that account around it, and who the account belongs to in the directory.</p>
    <div class="filters">
      <input type="search" id="w-q" placeholder="an account, a machine, an address, a reason…"
             title="Searches every event on the server, not the page in front of you: the account, who did it, the machine, the address, the program, what was changed and why. Case does not matter.">
      <select id="w-hours" title="How far back to look. This is a filter on what is shown and changes nothing about what is kept — Settings → The domain controllers says how long the events are kept.">
        <option value="0">all of it</option>
        <option value="1">last hour</option>
        <option value="24" selected>last 24 hours</option>
        <option value="168">last week</option>
        <option value="720">last month</option>
        <option value="8760">last year</option>
        <option value="custom" hidden>custom</option>
        <option value="pick">choose dates…</option>
      </select>
      <select id="w-event" title="One Windows event number on its own, where that is the question — 4740 is a lockout, 4625 a refused sign-in, 4624 a sign-in."><option value="">every event</option></select>
      <select id="w-server" title="Only what one controller wrote. All of them are read all the time; this only decides what is shown."><option value="">every controller</option></select>
      <select id="w-limit" title="How many rows are asked for at a time.">
        <option value="50">50 a page</option>
        <option value="100" selected>100 a page</option>
        <option value="250">250 a page</option>
      </select>
      <label class="check" title="A domain controller talks to every machine in the domain all day, and nine events in ten are one machine renewing a ticket. They are always read and always searchable; this decides whether they are drawn.">
        <input type="checkbox" id="w-machines"><span>machine accounts too</span></label>
      <button class="btn small" id="w-clear">Clear</button>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll rowsonly"><table class="rows" id="w-table" data-server-sort="1"><thead><tr id="w-head">
      </tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>
    <div class="center" id="w-empty" hidden>Nothing matches</div>
    <div class="pager" id="w-pager"></div>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="tab-events" hidden>
  <div class="card full" data-hold="1">
    <h2>What this daemon has done</h2>
    <p class="note">Every ban, every change of a setting, every sign-in, every conversation with
      the router. Kept in the database, so it survives a restart.</p>
    <div class="filters">
      <input type="search" id="ev-q" placeholder="anything in an event…"
             title="Searches every event this daemon has written down, not the page in front of you. Case does not matter.">
      <select id="ev-kind" title="Only one kind of event. Every ban, every change of a setting, every sign-in and every conversation with the router is written down here, which on a busy day is a lot of one kind and a little of another.">
        <option value="">everything</option>
        <option value="block">bans</option>
        <option value="watch">bans only watched</option>
        <option value="whitelist">the whitelist</option>
        <option value="mikrotik">the router</option>
        <option value="geolist">the country list</option>
        <option value="feeds">the public lists</option>
        <option value="geo">the country database</option>
        <option value="fold">folding</option>
        <option value="settings">settings</option>
        <option value="users">accounts</option>
        <option value="login">sign-ins</option>
        <option value="backup">backups</option>
        <option value="control">starting and stopping</option>
      </select>
      <select id="ev-limit" title="How many rows are asked for at a time. A bigger page is fewer presses and a slower answer; the pager underneath walks through the rest either way.">
        <option value="50">50 a page</option>
        <option value="100" selected>100 a page</option>
        <option value="250">250 a page</option>
      </select>
      <button class="btn small" id="ev-clear">Clear</button>
      <span class="muted small" id="ev-count" style="align-self:center"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll rowsonly"><table class="plain" id="ev-table" data-server-sort="1"><thead><tr>
      <th>When</th><th>Kind</th><th>Who</th><th>What</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>
    <div class="center" id="ev-empty" hidden>Nothing yet</div>
    <div class="pager" id="ev-pager"></div>
  </div>

  <div class="card full admin-only" style="margin-top:14px">
    <h2 title="The file under /var/log/isemon, as it stands. This is what the daemon said to itself — finer grained than the events above, and the place to look when something is not working rather than not happening. It is read, never written to, from this page.">The daemon's own log</h2>
    <p class="note">The tail of <span class="mono">/var/log/isemon/isemon.log</span>. Switch on
      <i>debug</i> in Settings to have it say everything — every decision, every command sent to
      the router, every read of the ISE logs.</p>
    <div class="btnrow"><button class="btn small" id="log-read">Read it</button>
      <select id="log-lines" title="How much of the daemon’s own log file to read. This is the file under /var/log/isemon, not the events above: it holds what the daemon said to itself, including the debug lines the events never carry."><option value="200">last 200 lines</option>
        <option value="600" selected>last 600</option><option value="2000">last 2000</option>
      </select></div>
    <div class="joblines said" id="log-out" style="margin-top:12px;max-height:460px"></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===================== Logs ===================== -->
<!-- Its own page, before Settings, and there whatever else is switched on: it used to
     be half of a row on the Overview, under the VPN's own cards, and went
     with them whenever another door was being looked at. -->
<section class="panel" id="tab-logs" hidden>
  <!-- `data-nodc`: a controller's name in a FILE PATH is not a controller
       turning up in a record, it is where its log file lives. Marking it
       there says nothing and makes a path hard to read. -->
  <div class="card full" data-nodc="1">
    <h2>The logs being read <span class="right small" id="ov-busy"></span></h2>
    <p class="note">Every log file this reads, how far and when. Both ISE nodes, always: only
      one of them is authenticating at any moment and which one changes without warning, so
      what comes out of both is put into one timeline before anything is counted. The token
      servers, when they are switched on, are read the same way and into the same timeline.
      Click a row to see where the file has been read up to.</p>
    <!-- The same columns `isemon --status` prints are here too (1.10.149):
         what is in it in words, when it was last read to the second, how
         much is not read yet and what the trouble is. The table names
         itself, so a layout somebody gave it outlives a column added. -->
    <div class="scroll"><table class="plain tight" id="ov-logs" data-cols-key="logs"><thead><tr>
      <th>File</th><th></th><th>What is in it</th><th class="num">Records</th><th class="num"
        title="How much the machine this file comes from wrote in the last 24 hours: the refusals and sign-ins it sent, or the events a domain controller wrote. A dash where it cannot be told - the token server's and eduroam's detail files are counted elsewhere.">Last 24 h</th><th>Read</th><th
        title="When something new was last read out of this file, to the second.">Last read</th><th>Behind</th><th class="num"
        title="How much of the file is written and not read yet.">Not read yet</th><th
        title="What is wrong with this file, in words: it is not there, it may not be read, its records are dated ahead of the clock.">Trouble</th>
    </tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===================== Settings ===================== -->
<section class="panel" id="tab-settings" hidden>
<div class="ctl-layout">

  <!-- the rail: one card at a time, and a way back to the others -->
  <nav class="ctl-nav" id="ctl-nav" aria-label="Settings sections">
    <div class="ctl-navtop">
      <button type="button" class="ctl-pin" id="ctl-pin" aria-pressed="false"
              aria-label="Keep this menu open"></button>
      <div class="ctl-find-wrap">
        <input type="search" id="ctl-find" placeholder="Find a setting…" autocomplete="off"
               aria-label="Find a setting">
        <div class="ctl-found" id="ctl-found" hidden></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="ctl-group">Logs read</div>
    <button type="button" data-pane="logs"    data-icon="log"    title="Where the two ISE nodes write, how often they are read, and which network devices' failures are allowed to earn a ban.">The ISE logs</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="otp"     data-icon="token"  title="The one-time password logs: whether they are read at all, and which folders the token servers write into. Off unless somebody switches it on, and off it changes nothing.">The OTP logs</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="eduroam" data-icon="wifi"   title="The eduroam logs: whether the visitors' network's own RADIUS server is read at all, which folders it writes into, and which accounts to leave out altogether. Off unless somebody switches it on, and off it changes nothing.">The eduroam logs</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="tacplus" data-icon="terminal" title="A TACACS+ daemon of this site's own — tac_plus rather than ISE — in front of the switches and routers: whether its logs are read at all, which folders it writes into, and what its two files are called. Off unless somebody switches it on, and off it changes nothing. Nothing read here is ever judged.">The tac_plus logs</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="nps" data-icon="wifi" title="A Windows NPS server — Network Policy Server, RADIUS that is not ISE — answering the switches, the wireless or the VPN: whether its logs are read at all, which folders they are in and which files are its. Off unless somebody switches it on, and off it changes nothing. Nothing read here is ever judged.">The NPS logs</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="radius" data-icon="wifi" title="A FreeRADIUS server of this site's own - not eduroam - answering the switches, the wireless or the VPN: whether its logs are read at all, which folders they are in, and which RADIUS clients are which door. Off unless somebody switches it on, and off it changes nothing.">The FreeRADIUS logs</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="winsec"  data-icon="shield" title="The Windows domain controllers' Security logs: whether they are read at all, which folders they write into, and how long the events are kept. Off unless somebody switches it on, and off it changes nothing. Nothing read here is ever judged and nothing here can earn anybody a ban.">The domain controllers</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="fwlog"   data-icon="net" title="A firewall's own log of what crossed it: whether it is read at all, which firewall it is, where its files are and how long what it says is kept. Switched on, a Traffic tab appears. Off unless somebody switches it on, and nothing read here is ever judged.">The firewall log</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="names"   data-icon="search" title="The names behind the addresses: which name servers to ask for a reverse lookup, in what order, and how long an answer is believed. Off unless somebody switches it on, and nothing on any page ever waits for it.">Names of addresses</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="rescan"  data-icon="history" title="Read every log from the beginning, once, and weigh what is in it against the settings as they stand. The one button here that reaches into the past.">Go back over the logs</button>
    <div class="ctl-group">Banning</div>
    <button type="button" data-pane="banning" data-icon="ban"    title="What earns a ban, how long it lasts, and the ladder it climbs.">Banning</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="white"   data-icon="shield" title="The addresses and ranges nothing here may ever ban — your own offices, your own concentrators, anything that must not be shut out by a mistake.">Whitelist<span class="n" id="white-n"></span></button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="lists"   data-icon="list"   title="Public blocklists other people keep: which are fetched, how often, and what an address being on one means here.">Public lists</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="updates" data-icon="refresh" title="How often the public lists and the country database are fetched again, at what time of day, and when each of them is next due.">Automatic updates</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="fold"    data-icon="fold"   title="Turning a street full of separate offenders into one subnet, and undoing that again.">Folding</button>
    <div class="ctl-group">The firewall</div>
    <button type="button" data-pane="router"  data-icon="router" title="How this signs in to the Mikrotik — or to both of them, where there is a pair — which address list it may touch, and the buttons that write to it or take ours off it.">Reaching the firewall</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="geo"     data-icon="globe"  title="The country database behind every flag on this page, and the country address list this keeps on the router.">GeoIP</button>
    <div class="ctl-group">Access</div>
    <button type="button" data-pane="people"  data-icon="people" title="Who may open this dashboard, what each of them may do, and who is signed in right now.">Accounts</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="dir"     data-icon="key"    title="Signing in with Active Directory: which server, how it is reached, and which groups get which role.">Active Directory</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="dir2"    data-icon="people" title="A second directory, asked only who somebody is when the first one does not have that name. Nobody ever signs in against it.">A second directory</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="tls"     data-icon="lock"   title="The certificate the dashboard answers with - uploaded, requested from your CA, or made here - and how it speaks TLS. Root puts it in, and nginx is asked before it is reloaded.">Certificates and TLS</button>
    <button type="button" class="admin-only" data-pane="acme" data-icon="refresh" title="The dashboard's certificate ordered from Let's Encrypt (or another ACME CA) and renewed by itself.">Automatic certificates</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="fw"      data-icon="wall"   title="Who may reach the dashboard, the published ban list and the health check, and the machine's own firewall: what isemon needs let in by itself, and rules of your own. Nothing is changed until it is saved here; root puts it in.">Firewall and access</button>
    <div class="ctl-group">Server</div>
    <button type="button" data-pane="network" data-icon="net" title="This server's address, gateway, DNS servers, host name and IPv6 - put in by the server itself, on trial: unless it is kept within minutes it goes back to the old one by itself.">Network</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="time" data-icon="clock" title="The clock of this server: chrony, its time servers, how far off it is - every time on these pages and every record read is worked out from it.">Time</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="power" data-icon="power" title="Restart or switch off this server.">Restart and power off</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="osup" data-icon="download" title="The operating system's packages updated by dnf (or apt), with what it says as it goes; a restart offered when one is needed.">System updates</button>
    <button type="button" class="admin-only" data-pane="sshkeys" data-icon="key" title="The keys that may sign in to this server as root over SSH: made here, added or taken away.">SSH keys of root</button>
    <div class="ctl-group">Data</div>
    <button type="button" data-pane="db"      data-icon="db"     title="Which database everything lives in - a file on this machine, MariaDB or PostgreSQL - how big it is, copies of it, and forgetting old records.">The database</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="backup" data-icon="archive" title="Backups of everything to another machine - FTP, FTPS, SFTP, SCP, SMB or a folder - locked with a password, on a schedule, and putting one back.">Backups</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="carry"   data-icon="download" title="Everything that makes this installation what it is, as one file to carry to another machine.">Export and import</button>
    <div class="ctl-group">Monitoring and updates</div>
    <button type="button" data-pane="upgrade" data-icon="download" title="A new version of isemon, put in from this page: send the signed package, press Upgrade, and root runs its install.sh --upgrade.">Upgrades</button>
    <button type="button" data-pane="about"   data-icon="info"   title="Version, uptime, what is being read and how long the last read took.">About</button>
  </nav>

  <div class="ctl-main">
  <div class="card full" id="readonly-note" hidden>
    <h2>You may look, but not change</h2>
    <p class="note" style="margin:0">This account is <b>read only</b>. Everything here is shown as
      it stands and nothing will save. A full administrator can change that under
      <i>Accounts</i>.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ---------- the ISE logs, banning, lists, folding ---------- -->
  <div class="ctl-pane" id="pane-logs">
    <div class="ctl-head">
      <h2 class="ctl-title">The ISE logs</h2>
      <p class="ctl-lede">Both nodes of the pair are read all the time, because which one is
        doing the authenticating changes without warning. Everything read is written down; the
        settings here decide which of it may earn somebody a ban.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full"><div id="sect-logs"></div></div>
  </div>

  <!-- The other thing in front of the VPN on a site that asks for a token.
       A page of its own rather than a card inside the ISE logs: it is a
       different system, written by different software, and reading it is a
       decision on its own. Off unless somebody switches it on. -->
  <div class="ctl-pane" id="pane-otp" hidden>
    <div class="ctl-head">
      <h2 class="ctl-title">The OTP logs</h2>
      <p class="ctl-lede">A one-time password is the other half of a VPN sign-in on a site
        that asks for one, and what the token server said is in no ISE log anywhere: somebody
        whose password was right and whose token was refused looks, in ISE alone, like somebody
        who simply did not finish.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full">
      <p class="note">Switched on, these are read the same way the ISE logs are — two servers
        if there are two, read into one timeline — and they get a tab of their own under
        Logins and under Failed attempts.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>Nothing here is ever judged.</b> The address on one of these records
        is the machine that asked the token server, which on every installation seen so far is
        the token server itself; banning on it would ban the server. Switched off, not one file
        is opened and nothing anywhere behaves differently.</p>
      <div id="sect-otp"></div>
    </div>
    <!-- Encrypted receiving (1.10.245; for otp 1.0.172's Settings → isemon →
         Encrypted sending): rsyslog's TLS input on this machine, put in by
         root's helper. The plain input on 514 is never touched. -->
    <div class="card full wr" id="otptls-card">
      <h2>Encrypted receiving (TLS)</h2>
      <p class="note">otp's nodes can send their totp.cgi and radius-detail lines here encrypted
        (syslog over TLS, RFC 5425). With mutual TLS only otp's own nodes may send. The plain
        input on 514 keeps working for any node not switched over yet, and what arrives is filed
        in the same folders - nothing about reading the logs changes.</p>
      <div id="ot-host"></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- A TACACS+ daemon of the site's own. A page of its own for the same
       reason the token logs have one: it is another system altogether,
       switched off on most installations, and switched off it must cost
       nothing and say nothing. -->
  <div class="ctl-pane" id="pane-tacplus" hidden>
    <div class="ctl-head">
      <h2 class="ctl-title">The tac_plus logs</h2>
      <p class="ctl-lede">Not every site puts its switches behind ISE. Where a TACACS+
        daemon of your own answers them — <span class="mono">tac_plus</span>, or the
        <span class="mono">tac_plus-ng</span> that came after it — this reads what it
        wrote: who signed in to which switch, from where, and every command they ran
        once they were in.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full">
      <p class="note">Switched on, a <b>TAC_Plus</b> button appears on Logins and on
        Failed attempts, beside the TACACS+ one that is ISE's. The two are never mixed:
        either daemon may be in front of any switch, and which of them answered is most
        of what somebody wants to know.</p>
      <p class="note">Two files are looked for in each folder. The <b>accounting log</b>
        is what people did — one line per command, and on some installations one per
        session. The <b>daemon's own log</b> beside it is the only place a sign-in that
        was <i>turned away</i> is written down; leave its name empty where there is
        none. Both are read by one reader that answers for what a line is, so a site
        writing both into one file need say nothing about it.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>Nothing here earns a ban</b> unless <i>Logins to network devices</i>
        is switched on under Banning → Where the bans come from (1.10.194), and then only
        from an address on the internet: the address on one of these lines is as often as
        not the jump host the whole of the network staff comes through. Switched off, not
        one file is opened and nothing anywhere behaves differently.</p>
      <div id="sect-tacplus"></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- A Windows NPS: RADIUS that is not ISE. A page of its own for the same
       reason tac_plus has one: another system altogether, switched off on
       most installations, and switched off it must cost nothing. -->
  <div class="ctl-pane" id="pane-fwlog" hidden>
    <div class="ctl-head">
      <h2 class="ctl-title">The firewall log</h2>
      <p class="ctl-lede">The firewall writes down every session it lets through or refuses,
        what its web filter and its SSL inspection decided, and who signed in to its VPN and
        to the firewall itself. Read here, that becomes the <b>Traffic</b> tab: what crossed
        it, to where, how much, and what was turned away.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full">
      <p class="note"><b>FortiGate</b> is read from its own syslog lines - <i>config log
        syslogd setting</i> pointed at this machine's syslog server, <i>format default</i>.
        Each line is <code>key=value</code> pairs; every one of them is kept and shown when a
        row is pressed, and the ones the page is built from become columns. The firewall's
        own clock to the nanosecond is the time of every line, so nothing here depends on
        a zone.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>Cisco FTD and ASA</b> are read from their own syslog messages -
        <code>%FTD-6-302014: Teardown TCP connection&hellip;</code> - however the syslog
        server files them: all in one file, or a file per message id
        (<code>%FTD-6-302014.log</code>), every one of which is followed. A session is
        counted once, when it ends, with how long it lasted, how much it carried (both ways
        together: FTD does not say which way) and why it ended; the line that opened it lends
        it its direction and its address translation. Refusals by access group or by a
        downloadable ACL, packets dropped on the way, the remote-access VPN and the
        site-to-site tunnels, failover, threat detection, and the management centre's file
        and intrusion events are all read. For a pair in failover give <b>one</b> firewall a
        folder for each unit: only the active unit's sessions are counted, and the standby's
        copies of them are left out. The time is the syslog header's, read in the zone
        of the firewall's clock - set beside each firewall below; an FTD writes UTC unless
        told otherwise, and one whose lines keep arriving whole hours old is said so
        here.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>MikroTik RouterOS</b> is read from its own syslog lines - a
        <i>/system logging action</i> of type <i>remote</i> pointed at this machine, and a
        rule for each topic wanted: <code>firewall</code>, <code>system</code>,
        <code>account</code>, <code>ssh</code>, <code>ovpn</code>, <code>l2tp</code>,
        <code>sstp</code>, <code>dhcp</code>. However the syslog server files them - all in
        one file, or a file per topic (<code>firewall,info.log</code>) - every file is
        followed. A firewall line says only that a rule logged the packet, never what became
        of it: that is read off the rule's <i>log prefix</i> - <code>DROP</code>,
        <code>REJECT</code>, <code>DENY</code>, <code>BLOCK</code> is a refusal,
        <code>ACCEPT</code>, <code>ALLOW</code>, <code>PERMIT</code> a pass, anything else
        is shown as logged. Sign-ins to the router (winbox, ssh, the web, the API), every
        change to its configuration and who made it, the VPN of people (OpenVPN, L2TP, SSTP,
        PPTP: who, from where, which address inside) and the tunnels to other sites are read
        as well; a refused sign-in from the internet can earn a ban like any other at the
        firewall. The time is the syslog header's, read in the zone set beside the
        firewall.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>Read beside ISE.</b> Where the ISE logs are read too, each says what
        the other cannot: a VPN sign-in FTD refused as <code>*****</code> is given the name
        ISE recorded for the same attempt, the person on a firewall line is shown with their
        last ISE sign-in, and a name looked up at the top shows what the firewall saw of it.
        Either works on its own.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>How much is kept.</b> A small office's firewall writes three
        million lines a day, nearly all of them sessions it let through. Those are kept as
        rows for a day by default; everything it refused, blocked or said about itself for a
        week. Every card and the chart count all of it either way - they are counted as the
        lines arrive - so shortening how long the rows are kept never makes the numbers
        smaller.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>Nothing here earns a ban</b> unless <i>Logins at the firewall
        itself</i> is switched on under Banning → Where the bans come from, and then only a
        sign-in refused from an address on the internet. Switched off, not one file is
        opened and nothing anywhere behaves differently.</p>
      <div id="sect-fwlog"></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="ctl-pane" id="pane-nps" hidden>
    <div class="ctl-head">
      <h2 class="ctl-title">The NPS logs</h2>
      <p class="ctl-lede">Not every site's RADIUS is ISE. Where a Windows <b>Network Policy
        Server</b> answers the switches, the wireless or the VPN, this reads the log it
        writes of every request and every answer: which machine on which socket or radio,
        which person on the VPN, what NPS decided and why.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full">
      <p class="note">Switched on, buttons appear on Logins and on Failed attempts beside
        ISE's — <b>NPS VPN</b>, <b>NPS 802.1X LAN</b>, <b>NPS 802.1X Wi-Fi</b>,
        <b>NPS MAB LAN</b>, <b>NPS MAB Wi-Fi</b> — and cards on the Overview. Each of them
        appears only once NPS has really answered something through that door: a site
        whose NPS has no VPN behind it is never shown an NPS VPN button.</p>
      <p class="note">NPS has to write its log as <b>ODBC (Legacy)</b> — <i>Accounting →
        Log file properties</i> on the NPS server — with both <i>authentication requests
        and responses</i> ticked. A request and its answer are two lines, and they are
        read together: the answer says yes or no and why, and only the request says
        which socket, which radio and which MAC. The switches' own "is the server there"
        probes are recognised and left out. <span class="mono">docs/nps-logs.md</span>
        says how to get the files here.</p>
      <p class="note">Only a <b>VPN refusal</b> is ever judged here - while <i>The VPN through
        a Windows NPS</i> under Banning → Where the bans come from is on, as it is unless
        switched off. A socket or a radio never earns a ban.
        Switched off, not one file is opened and nothing anywhere behaves
        differently.</p>
      <div id="sect-nps"></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- A FreeRADIUS of the site's own (1.10.203): the doors ISE and NPS
       answer, answered by FreeRADIUS - not eduroam, which is its own pane. -->
  <div class="ctl-pane" id="pane-radius" hidden>
    <div class="ctl-head">
      <h2 class="ctl-title">The FreeRADIUS logs</h2>
      <p class="ctl-lede">Where a <b>FreeRADIUS</b> server of the site's own answers the
        switches, the wireless or the VPN - MAB and 802.1X on the LAN and on Wi-Fi, and the
        VPN - this reads its <code>radius.log</code>: who was let in or refused,
        through which RADIUS client and from which machine, and what it said. Not eduroam,
        which has a pane of its own.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full">
      <p class="note">Switched on, buttons appear on Logins and on Failed attempts -
        <b>FreeRADIUS VPN</b>, <b>FreeRADIUS 802.1X LAN</b>, <b>FreeRADIUS 802.1X
        Wi-Fi</b>, <b>FreeRADIUS MAB LAN</b>, <b>FreeRADIUS MAB Wi-Fi</b> - and cards on the
        Overview, each only once something has come through that door.</p>
      <p class="note"><code>radius.log</code> does not say which door a
        sign-in came through, so it is worked out: a calling station that is an
        <b>address</b> is the VPN, and so is anything through a client named below as the
        VPN; a name that is the machine's own <b>MAC</b> is MAB, everything else 802.1X;
        and <b>LAN or Wi-Fi</b> is the client's, as named below, or else what the
        <b>accounting</b> files said of that machine - its port type, or a network name
        on a radio. What is still unknown is shown as <i>FreeRADIUS other</i>.
        <code>radius.log</code> must log the sign-ins:
        <code>auth = yes</code> in <code>radiusd.conf</code>.
        A password FreeRADIUS writes into the log
        (<code>auth_badpass</code>) is never kept.
        <code>docs/freeradius-logs.md</code> says how to get the files
        here.</p>
      <p class="note">Only a <b>VPN refusal</b> from an address on the internet can earn a ban
        - while <i>The VPN through a FreeRADIUS of your own</i> under Banning &rarr; Where the
        bans come from is on, which it is not until switched on. A socket or a radio never
        does. Switched off, not one file is opened and nothing anywhere behaves
        differently.</p>
      <div id="sect-radius"></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- The visitors' network, which is a different RADIUS server again -
       FreeRADIUS rather than ISE, writing two files rather than one. A page
       of its own for the same reason the token logs have one. -->
  <div class="ctl-pane" id="pane-eduroam" hidden>
    <div class="ctl-head">
      <h2 class="ctl-title">The eduroam logs</h2>
      <p class="ctl-lede">A site inside eduroam runs a RADIUS server of its own for the
        visitors' network — its own people signing in away from their desks, and other
        institutions' people signing in here. None of it goes anywhere near ISE.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full">
      <p class="note">Two files per server are read, and they say different things. The
        <b>authentication log</b> says who got in and who did not, and is where the rows come
        from. The <b>accounting files</b> are where the access point, the network, the VLAN,
        the address the machine was given and the name the machine calls itself are — the
        controller writes them minutes after the sign-in, so they are joined to the row by the
        machine's MAC when the page is drawn.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>FreeRADIUS or NPS.</b> <i>Which server writes the eduroam logs</i>
        says which. With FreeRADIUS it is the two files above. With a Windows NPS it is the
        one log NPS writes, in the <b>ODBC (Legacy)</b> format the NPS module reads: a request
        and its answer make one sign-in, and the accounting in the same file says where the
        machine was. Where NPS answers other things as well, <i>Which of NPS's answers are
        eduroam's</i> names the eduroam policies. <span class="mono">docs/eduroam-nps.md</span>
        says how to set NPS up.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>Nothing here is ever judged.</b> The only address on one of these
        lines is the wireless controller the request came through; banning it would take the
        whole of eduroam off the air. Switched off, not one file is opened and nothing
        anywhere behaves differently.</p>
      <p class="note">A password is never kept. FreeRADIUS writes what was offered on a
        refusal — <code>[somebody/hunter2]</code> — and the half after the slash is dropped
        where the line is read, so it is in no row, no record and no log this program
        writes.</p>
      <div id="sect-eduroam"></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- The Windows domain controllers, which are not a RADIUS server at all.
       Half of what a VPN refusal means is written here and nowhere else: the
       lockout, the machine it came from and the program holding the old
       password. Read only, judged never. -->
  <div class="ctl-pane" id="pane-winsec" hidden>
    <div class="ctl-head">
      <h2 class="ctl-title">The domain controllers</h2>
      <p class="ctl-lede">&quot;The VPN refused them four times&quot; and &quot;the domain
        locked the account at 08:12, from NB-NOVAK, through Outlook, because the password
        saved there is the old one&quot; are the same morning — and only one of them is in
        an ISE log anywhere.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full">
      <p class="note">Switched on, the Security logs are read the way the ISE logs are —
        one folder per controller, all of them read into one timeline — and a
        <b>Domain</b> tab appears beside the others: the lockouts with what led to each
        one, the patterns worth looking at, and every event itself, searchable and paged.
        Switched off, not one file is opened, the tab is not drawn, and nothing anywhere
        behaves differently.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>Nothing here earns a ban</b> unless <i>Domain logons from
        outside</i> is switched on under Banning → Where the bans come from, and then only a
        failed logon from an address on the internet. A VPN sign-in checked against Active
        Directory is ONE attempt: the ISE
        record and the domain's own event are two views of it, and counting both would
        double every person on every page. These events live in a table of their own, are
        never a row under Failed attempts or Logins, and the chart on the front page does
        not know they exist.</p>
      <p class="note">The logs get here with NXLog on each controller —
        <code>docs/nxlog.conf</code> is a working configuration and
        <code>docs/windows-security-log.md</code> says what to change in it. A controller
        that stops writing is said so on the About page with all the other logs.</p>
      <div id="sect-winsec"></div>
      <!-- What the Domain page's answers are counted out of, and the two
           things somebody may ask of it. Both existed only at a terminal,
           which is no use to whoever is looking at the page it is about. -->
      <div class="rollcard" id="winsec-roll" hidden>
        <h3 class="peek-h">Counted ahead of time</h3>
        <p class="note" id="winsec-roll-says"></p>
        <div class="btnrow admin-only">
          <button class="btn cmd" id="winsec-rollbuild" data-icon="refresh"
            title="Count every stored event again, from nothing, and prove the result before believing it. The database does the counting itself, so it is seconds rather than minutes; nothing else may be written while it runs. The page goes on working throughout - it simply asks the events themselves until this is done and proved.">Count
            them all again</button>
          <button class="btn cmd" id="winsec-rollcheck" data-icon="check"
            title="Read the counting back against the events it counted: every sum over the whole table, then the most recent hours line for line. Changes nothing. If they disagree the page goes straight back to asking the events themselves and says so here.">Read
            it back against the events</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="btnrow admin-only" style="margin-top:14px">
        <button class="btn cmd" id="winsec-dirread" data-icon="shield"
          title="Ask Active Directory everything again, now: the domain's own rules, its controllers, its trusts, its sites, who is in the groups that run it — everything the Domain page weighs an alarm against and everything the watching compares. Read only. Nothing stored here is read again, so it is one round of searches rather than minutes of work. This is exactly what the clock above does once a day.">Ask the
          directory again now</button>
        <button class="btn cmd" id="winsec-reread" data-icon="history">Read every stored
          event again</button>
      </div>
      <!-- The same bar as on the Domain page, off the same answer: whoever
           pressed the button is standing here, and a button that starts ten
           minutes of work with nothing to look at afterwards is a button
           nobody trusts the second time. -->
      <div class="going wide" id="winsec-going" hidden>
        <div class="bar"><span></span></div>
        <div class="says"></div>
      </div>
      <p class="btnhelp ro">Read only — nothing is ever written to Active Directory.
        Both buttons above only ask it questions.</p>
      <p class="btnhelp"><b>Ask the directory again now</b> does one round of searches and
        nothing else: the domain’s rules, its controllers, its trusts, its sites and who is
        in the groups that run it. Nothing stored here is read again. It is the same round
        the clock above makes once a day — that clock never re-reads the logs either.</p>
      <p class="btnhelp">What each event MEANS is worked out once, when the line is read,
        and kept beside the row — which controller it was, whether replicating the
        directory was expected. Name a controller here and the events already stored still
        carry yesterday's answer until they are read again. This does it now, in the
        background, and the Domain page catches up by itself. It happens on its own when
        these settings are saved; this is for watching it happen.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="ctl-pane" id="pane-banning" hidden>
    <div class="ctl-head">
      <h2 class="ctl-title">Banning</h2>
      <p class="ctl-lede">What earns a ban and how long it lasts. The ladder is the part worth
        reading twice: it is how a first offender gets ten minutes and somebody who keeps coming
        back ends up out for good.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full"><div id="sect-banning"></div></div>
    <!-- Where the bans come from (1.10.193). The switches are settings; the
         counts and the buttons beside them are drawn by `drawSources`. -->
    <div class="card full" id="sources-card">
      <h2>Where the bans come from <span class="right" id="sources-count"></span></h2>
      <p class="note">Every ban says what made it. One list of banned addresses goes to the
        firewalls, into the published list and into the access list, made of the bans of every
        source switched on here. Switched off, a source makes no new bans and the ones it made
        are carried nowhere - they are kept, and run out as they would, and switched on again
        they are back. An address banned by two sources is carried while either is on. Only
        addresses on the internet are banned by the sources that count logins at devices, at
        the firewall or at the domain: the bans go to a firewall in front of the network.</p>
      <div id="sect-sources"></div>
      <div id="sources-box" style="margin-top:12px"></div>
    </div>
    <!-- The ban list, published at a URL for a firewall this machine never
         logs in to. The settings are the daemon's as everywhere else; the
         URL, the fetches and the guide are drawn by `drawBanfeed`. -->
    <div class="card full" id="banfeed-card">
      <h2>Publish the ban list for other firewalls <span class="right" id="banfeed-count"></span></h2>
      <p class="note">A Cisco FTD (through its FMC) or a FortiGate fetches the addresses being
        kept out from a URL, by itself, every few minutes. Nothing here logs in to those
        firewalls and they do not sign in here: the URL is all they need. Who may fetch it is
        the web server's allow list, and the addresses under <i>Only these may fetch it</i>.</p>
      <div id="sect-banfeed"></div>
      <div id="banfeed-box" class="banfeed-box">
        <p class="note" id="banfeed-off">Switch it on above and save, and the URL appears here.</p>
        <div id="banfeed-on" hidden>
          <div class="banfeed-url-label">The URL for <b id="banfeed-for">…</b>
            <span class="dim">— click it to copy</span></div>
          <button type="button" class="banfeed-url mono" id="banfeed-url"
                  title="Click to copy the URL to the clipboard, ready to paste into the firewall.">…</button>
          <div id="banfeed-md5-wrap" hidden>
            <div class="banfeed-url-label">The MD5 URL, which the FMC asks for beside it
              <span class="dim">— click it to copy</span></div>
            <button type="button" class="banfeed-url mono" id="banfeed-md5"
                    title="Click to copy. The FMC fetches this first and fetches the list only when it has changed.">…</button>
          </div>
          <div class="kv" id="banfeed-basic-row" hidden><span class="k">The firewall signs in as</span>
            <span class="v" id="banfeed-basic"></span></div>
          <div class="kv"><span class="k">Fetched by</span><span class="v" id="banfeed-seen">…</span></div>
          <div class="kv" id="banfeed-refused-row" hidden><span class="k">Refused</span>
            <span class="v" id="banfeed-refused"></span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="note" id="banfeed-readonly" hidden>Only an administrator is shown the URL:
          the key in it is the whole of what keeps the list private.</p>
        <div class="btnrow">
          <button class="btn cmd primary" id="banfeed-copy" data-icon="copy"
                  title="Copy the URL to the clipboard.">Copy the URL</button>
          <button class="btn cmd" id="banfeed-guide" data-icon="info"
                  title="How to set the chosen firewall up to fetch the list, step by step, with its commands.">How to set it up on the firewall…</button>
          <button class="btn cmd" id="banfeed-open" data-icon="eye"
                  title="The list exactly as the firewall is given it, in the format chosen above.">See what it gets</button>
          <button class="btn cmd admin-only" id="banfeed-newkey" data-icon="key"
                  title="Make a new key. The URL changes, and every firewall still fetching the old one is refused from that moment.">New key…</button>
          <button class="btn cmd admin-only" id="banfeed-forget" data-icon="forget"
                  title="Forget who has fetched the list and the refusals: the lines under Fetched by start again from nothing. The list, its URL and every firewall's access stay exactly as they are - the next fetch is simply written down as the first.">Clear who fetched it…</button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Going back over the logs. A page of its own rather than a card at the
       bottom of Banning: it is the one button on this whole dashboard that
       reaches into the past and changes what is kept out, and a button like
       that should not be found by scrolling past six settings about
       something else. -->
  <div class="ctl-pane" id="pane-rescan" hidden>
    <div class="ctl-head">
      <h2 class="ctl-title">Go back over the logs</h2>
      <p class="ctl-lede">Read every log from the beginning, once, and weigh what is in it
        against the settings as they stand — including the rotated copies and the days before
        this was installed. A ban applied by hand to something that happened last week is a ban
        nobody can defend; the same ban applied by the same rule is the one the daemon would
        have made at the time, only late.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full">
      <h2>What it would weigh <span class="right" id="rescan-when"></span></h2>
      <div class="kv"><span class="k">The rule it weighs by</span>
        <span class="v" id="rescan-rule">…</span></div>
      <div class="kv"><span class="k">Only attempts newer than</span>
        <span class="v" id="rescan-limit">…</span></div>
      <div class="kv"><span class="k">What is in the database</span>
        <span class="v" id="rescan-note">…</span></div>
      <div class="kv"><span class="k">The logs it reads</span>
        <span class="v" id="rescan-logs">…</span></div>
      <p class="note">Every log that is switched on is read from the beginning - its live file,
        every rotated copy and every day still in its folder - whichever kind of log it is.
        Untick one to leave it out. A log written without a zone in it (everything but ISE)
        whose zone was <b>changed</b> since its records were read is left out, and the pass
        says so: read again it would file each line a second time at the new moment. Erase
        that module's records instead, which reads it again in the new zone.
        Logins at the firewall, to network devices, to the domain and the VPN through a
        FreeRADIUS are weighed over the same span as well, where they are switched on under
        Banning.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>Nothing is stored twice</b>, and the pass says how many it read and how
        many were new.</p>
      <p class="note">Nothing is un-banned and nothing is banned twice either. An address on the
        whitelist is left alone, a device outside <span class="mono">watch_devices</span> earns
        nothing, and an address already kept out is simply skipped.</p>
      <div class="btnrow">
        <button class="btn cmd primary admin-only" id="rescan-go" data-icon="history"
                title="Read every log file from the beginning and apply the bans the records earn. It runs in the background and shows you what it is doing.">Go back over every log…</button>
        <select id="rescan-days" style="min-width:190px"
                title="How far back to weigh. Everything is still read and written down; this only limits what may earn a ban.">
          <option value="0">weigh everything there is</option>
          <option value="1">only the last day</option>
          <option value="7">only the last week</option>
          <option value="30">only the last month</option>
          <option value="90">only the last three months</option>
        </select>
      </div>
      <p class="btnhelp">The same from the server:
        <span class="mono">isemon --rescan</span> ·
        <span class="mono">isemon --rescan --days 7</span> ·
        <span class="mono">isemon --rescan --no-reread</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="ctl-pane" id="pane-white" hidden>
    <div class="ctl-head">
      <h2 class="ctl-title">Whitelist</h2>
      <p class="ctl-lede">The one list nothing in this daemon may overrule. An address, a range
        or a whole country on it is never banned — not by the counting, not by a public list,
        not by somebody pressing <i>Ban</i>, and nothing of it is ever sent to the router. Put
        your own offices, your own VPN concentrators and your monitoring here before anything
        else.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full">
      <h2>Never banned <span class="right" id="white-count"></span></h2>
      <p class="btnhelp" id="white-countries" hidden></p>
      <p class="note">Adding one lets go of anything already banned inside it, at the same
        moment — a list that says "never" beside a rule on the router that says otherwise is a
        pair nobody can read. A range covers everything in it: <span class="mono">10.0.0.0/8</span>
        spares every address in it, and <span class="mono">2a01:430::/32</span> does the same for
        IPv6.</p>
      <div class="btnrow admin-only" style="margin-bottom:14px">
        <input id="white-ip" type="text" style="min-width:250px" autocomplete="off"
               placeholder="192.168.0.0/16 or 2a01:430::/32"
               title="One address, or a whole range written as a prefix. Everything inside a range is spared, which is how a whole office goes on in one line. An address here is never banned by anything: not by the counting, not by a public list, and not by somebody pressing Ban.">
        <input id="white-note" type="text" style="min-width:230px" autocomplete="off"
               placeholder="what it is — “the north office”"
               title="What this range is, in your own words. It is shown beside the entry for ever after, which is the difference between a whitelist somebody can prune in a year and one nobody dares touch.">
        <button class="btn cmd primary admin-only" id="white-add" data-icon="shield"
                title="Put this address or range beyond the reach of the banning, for good. Anything banned inside it comes off the list and off the router at the same moment.">Never ban this</button>
      </div>
      <div id="white-list"></div>
      <div class="center" id="white-empty" hidden>Nothing is whitelisted yet</div>
      <p class="btnhelp">The same from the server:
        <span class="mono">isemon --allow 192.168.0.0/16 --why "the office"</span> ·
        <span class="mono">isemon --whitelist</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full"><div id="sect-white"></div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="ctl-pane" id="pane-lists" hidden>
    <div class="ctl-head">
      <h2 class="ctl-title">Public lists</h2>
      <p class="ctl-lede">Ranges other people have already decided are trouble.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full"><div id="sect-lists"></div></div>
    <div class="card full">
      <h2>Lists somebody else keeps <span class="right" id="feeds-when"></span></h2>
      <p class="note">Nothing is banned for being on one of these alone — four thousand ranges
        are not a firewall rule and most of them will never come near this VPN — but the first one
        that <b>does</b> fail a login here goes on the list for good. Start with FireHOL level 1,
        which is chosen to have no false positives.</p>
      <div id="feeds-list"></div>
      <p class="btnhelp">A list switched on or off here is saved with <b>Save the changes</b>
        at the bottom of the page, with everything else changed on it.</p>
      <div class="btnrow" style="margin-top:12px">
        <button class="btn cmd admin-only" id="feeds-fetch" data-icon="download"
                title="Fetch every switched-on list now rather than waiting for the next round. Each one is a few hundred kilobytes; the whole set takes under a minute.">Fetch them all now</button>
        <span class="muted small" id="feeds-note" style="align-self:center"></span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="ctl-pane" id="pane-updates" hidden>
    <div class="ctl-head">
      <h2 class="ctl-title">Automatic updates</h2>
      <p class="ctl-lede">A blocklist a year old is a list of addresses that have since been
        cleaned up, and a country database a year old puts the wrong flag beside half the page.
        Both are fetched again by themselves; this is how often, and at what time of day.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full"><div id="sect-updates"></div></div>
    <div class="card full">
      <h2>What is fetched, and when it is next due
        <span class="right" id="updates-when"></span></h2>
      <p class="note">Only the lists that are switched on are fetched. A fetch that fails is
        tried again at the next round rather than given up on, and how many times in a row it has
        failed is kept — a list that has been refusing for a week is worth knowing about, and one
        that failed once last night is not.</p>
      <div class="scroll"><table class="plain" id="updates-table"><thead><tr>
        <th>What</th><th class="num">Ranges</th><th>Last fetched</th><th>Next, by itself</th>
        <th>State</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>
      <div class="center" id="updates-empty" hidden>Nothing is switched on to be fetched</div>
      <div class="btnrow" style="margin-top:12px">
        <button class="btn cmd primary admin-only" id="updates-now" data-icon="refresh"
                title="Fetch every switched-on list and, if it is on, the country database — now, rather than waiting for the next round. It shows what it is doing as it goes.">Fetch everything now</button>
        <span class="muted small" id="updates-note" style="align-self:center"></span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="ctl-pane" id="pane-fold" hidden>
    <div class="ctl-head">
      <h2 class="ctl-title">Folding into subnets</h2>
      <p class="ctl-lede">Attacks arrive from whole streets. Folding turns a dozen banned
        addresses in one /24 into one line here and one rule on the router — and every folding can
        be undone on its own afterwards.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full"><div id="sect-fold"></div></div>
    <div class="card full">
      <h2>Fold now</h2>
      <p class="note">It says what it would do before it does anything, and a subnet is only made
        where two or more banned addresses fall inside it — never where it would swallow something
        under <i>never kept out</i>.</p>
      <div class="btnrow">
        <button class="btn cmd admin-only" id="fold-open" data-icon="fold"
                title="Work out what folding would do right now and show it, address by address, before anything is changed.">Look at what it would do</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ---------- the router ---------- -->
  <div class="ctl-pane" id="pane-router" hidden>
    <div class="ctl-head">
      <h2 class="ctl-title">Reaching the firewall</h2>
      <p class="ctl-lede">This reaches the firewall and touches two lists on it and nothing else
        — on a Mikrotik over ssh, two address lists; on an OPNsense or a pfSense two aliases,
        through its API or over ssh. Choose which kind each firewall is: only what that kind needs
        is asked for, and what was set for another kind is kept for switching back. Where there
        is a pair in high availability, switch the second one on below: both are then written to
        in the same pass and kept saying exactly the same thing, each with its own address,
        account and key.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full"><div id="sect-router"></div></div>
    <div class="card full">
      <h2><span id="mt-title">The first Mikrotik</span> <span class="right" id="mt-when"></span></h2>
      <p class="note">A key is better than a password: the key never leaves this machine and the
        router holds only its public half.</p>
      <div id="mt-state"></div>
      <div class="btnrow" style="margin-top:12px">
        <button class="btn cmd" id="mt-guide" data-icon="info"
                title="How to set the kind of firewall chosen above up for this machine, step by step, with its commands: the account, the lists, the rule that drops what is on them.">How to set it up on the firewall…</button>
      </div>
      <div class="subpanel" id="mt-testbox" style="margin-top:16px" hidden>
        <div class="btnrow">
          <span class="spacer"></span>
          <button class="btn cmd primary" id="mt-test2" data-icon="pulse"
                  title="Reach the firewall and read its aliases. Nothing is written and nothing is changed.">Try reaching it</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="subpanel" id="mt-keybox" style="margin-top:16px">
        <p class="note" id="mt-keynote" title="The key is made here and never leaves this machine except when you download it. Put the public half on the firewall — on RouterOS, /user ssh-keys import public-key-file=… user=… — and it will let this in without a password. Keep the private half safe: anybody holding it can sign in to the firewall as that account.">Made here, and it never leaves this machine except when you download it. Put the
          <b>public</b> half on the firewall.</p>
        <div class="btnrow">
          <button class="btn cmd admin-only" id="mt-keygen" data-icon="key"
                  title="Make a fresh ed25519 pair here. The private half stays on this machine and the public half is shown below for you to put on the router. Any key already stored is replaced, so until the new public half is on the router this will not be able to sign in.">Make a key</button>
          <button class="btn cmd admin-only" id="mt-paste" data-icon="upload"
                  title="Use a private key you already have. Paste it whole, including its BEGIN and END lines; its public half is worked out here so you do not have to hand that over as well.">Use my own</button>
          <button class="btn cmd admin-only" id="mt-download" data-icon="download"
                  title="Download the private half as a file, for keeping somewhere safe or for using the same key elsewhere. It is written into Events that it was downloaded, and by whom.">Download it</button>
          <button class="btn cmd danger admin-only" id="mt-forget" data-icon="forget"
                  title="Delete the private key from this machine. Unless the router is set to take a password instead, nothing here will reach it until a new key is made and put on it.">Forget it</button>
          <span class="spacer"></span>
          <button class="btn cmd primary" id="mt-test" data-icon="pulse"
                  title="Sign in to the router and ask it its own name. Nothing is written and nothing is changed — it is the quickest way to find out whether the address, the account and the key are right.">Try reaching it</button>
        </div>
        <div id="mt-key" style="margin-top:12px"></div>
      </div>

    </div>

    <div class="card full">
      <h2><span id="mt2-title">The second Mikrotik</span> <span class="right" id="mt2-when"></span></h2>
      <p class="note">A second firewall, kept saying exactly the same thing as the first. For a pair in
        high availability.</p>
      <div id="sect-router2"></div>
      <div id="mt2-state" style="margin-top:14px"></div>
      <div class="btnrow" style="margin-top:12px">
        <button class="btn cmd" id="mt2-guide" data-icon="info"
                title="How to set the kind of firewall chosen above up for this machine, step by step, with its commands: the account, the lists, the rule that drops what is on them.">How to set it up on the firewall…</button>
      </div>
      <div class="subpanel" id="mt2-testbox" style="margin-top:16px" hidden>
        <div class="btnrow">
          <span class="spacer"></span>
          <button class="btn cmd primary" id="mt2-test2" data-icon="pulse">Try reaching it</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="subpanel" id="mt2-keybox" style="margin-top:16px">
        <p class="note" id="mt2-keynote" title="A key of its own, made here and never leaving this machine except when you download it. Put the public half on the second firewall — the two have a key each on purpose, so replacing one cannot lock this out of both.">A key of its own, for the second firewall. Put the <b>public</b> half on it.</p>
        <div class="btnrow">
          <button class="btn cmd admin-only" id="mt2-keygen" data-icon="key">Make a key</button>
          <button class="btn cmd admin-only" id="mt2-paste" data-icon="upload">Use my own</button>
          <button class="btn cmd admin-only" id="mt2-download" data-icon="download">Download
            it</button>
          <button class="btn cmd danger admin-only" id="mt2-forget" data-icon="forget">Forget
            it</button>
          <span class="spacer"></span>
          <button class="btn cmd primary" id="mt2-test" data-icon="pulse">Try reaching it</button>
        </div>
        <div id="mt2-key" style="margin-top:12px"></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card full">
      <h2>Writing to them</h2>
      <p class="note">Both of these act on every firewall that is switched on, in one pass.</p>
      <div class="btnrow admin-only">
        <button class="btn cmd primary" id="mt-sync" data-icon="router"
                title="Write every address list to every firewall now: the banned addresses, and the chosen countries if that is switched on. Only entries carrying our own mark are added or removed.">Sync every address list now</button>
        <button class="btn cmd danger" id="mt-purge" data-icon="broom"
                title="Take the entries this daemon put on the firewall(s) off them — and nothing else. Anything you added by hand, every other address list, and all your firewall rules are untouched.">Remove our address lists…</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card full">
      <h2>An access list for a Cisco router</h2>
      <p class="note">For a router this machine does <b>not</b> sign in to. The Mikrotik above is
        kept in step over ssh, second by second; a Cisco somebody else looks after is not
        something to be logged in to from here, so what it gets is a file — made at the moment
        somebody asks for it on the <i>Firewall</i> page, out of the addresses that are banned
        <b>for good</b>, and pasted by a person who is looking at it.</p>
      <p class="note">Everything above the ending is this machine's: one deny line per address,
        written afresh every time. The ending is yours, and it is the same body of an access
        list you would type at the router — as many lines as your own list needs. Every line is
        read before it is saved, and a line a router would refuse is refused here instead, where
        it costs nothing. An access list ends with an implicit deny, so a list with no permit at
        the bottom is a list that stops everything: that is why this is a setting and not
        something to remember by hand.</p>
      <div id="sect-ios"></div>
      <div class="btnrow" style="margin-top:14px">
        <button class="btn cmd primary" id="ios-show" data-icon="list"
                title="The whole file as it stands this moment, both families, with the addresses that are out for good in it.">See the whole list…</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ---------- GeoIP ---------- -->
  <div class="ctl-pane" id="pane-geo" hidden>
    <div class="ctl-head">
      <h2 class="ctl-title">GeoIP</h2>
      <p class="ctl-lede">Which country each address belongs to. It is what puts a flag beside
        every address on every page, and it is what the router's country list is filled from. A
        country is never judged here: a scanner in Rotterdam looks exactly like an employee in
        Rotterdam, so acting on one is a rule you write on the router, deliberately.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full"><div id="sect-geo"></div></div>

    <div class="card full">
      <h2>The country database <span class="right" id="geo-when"></span></h2>
      <p class="note">A free monthly list, about four hundred thousand ranges. The fetch takes a
        minute and replaces the whole of it, so what is here is one month's answer rather than
        several months stacked up.</p>
      <div id="geo-state"></div>
      <div class="btnrow" style="margin-top:12px">
        <button class="btn cmd admin-only" id="geo-fetch" data-icon="download"
                title="Fetch this month's country database now. It is about 100 MB compressed and replaces everything currently held; every flag on the page is redrawn from it afterwards.">Fetch it now</button>
        <span class="muted small" id="geo-note" style="align-self:center"></span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card full" id="geolist-card">
      <h2>The country list on the router <span class="right" id="geolist-when"></span></h2>
      <p class="note">A second list on the same firewall, holding whole countries rather than individual
        offenders.</p>
      <div id="geolist-state"></div>
      <div class="btnrow" style="margin-top:12px">
        <button class="btn cmd primary admin-only" id="geolist-sync" data-icon="globe"
                title="Send the chosen countries' ranges to the router now. Everything of ours already on that list comes off first, so it holds this month's ranges and not last month's as well; anything on it without our mark is left alone.">Fill it now</button>
        <button class="btn cmd danger admin-only" id="geolist-purge" data-icon="broom"
                title="Take our country ranges off the router and leave the list empty. The ban list is not touched, and a rule pointed at this list goes on working and simply matches nothing.">Empty it on the router…</button>
        <span class="muted small" id="geolist-note" style="align-self:center"></span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ---------- accounts ---------- -->
  <div class="ctl-pane" id="pane-people" hidden>
    <div class="ctl-head">
      <h2 class="ctl-title">Accounts</h2>
      <p class="ctl-lede">Three roles. A <b>full admin</b> may change anything: ban, unban,
        change settings, talk to the router, make accounts. A <b>ban admin</b> may look at every
        page, ban an address, let one back in and put the lists on the firewall — and nothing
        else. A <b>read only</b> account may look at every page and change nothing — no ban, no
        setting, not even a fetch.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full"><div id="sect-people"></div></div>
    <div class="card full">
      <h2>Who may open this <span class="right" id="users-count"></span></h2>
      <p class="note">The accounts that may open this dashboard, and what each of them may do.</p>
      <div id="users-list"></div>
      <div class="btnrow admin-only" style="margin-top:12px">
        <button class="btn cmd primary" id="user-add" data-icon="add"
                title="Make an account somebody else can sign in with. You choose the first password and they are asked to change it.">Make an account…</button>
        <button class="btn cmd" id="sessions-show" data-icon="eye"
                title="Every sign-in that is currently open, with where it came from and when it was last used — and a way to end them all.">Who is signed in…</button>
      </div>
      <p class="btnhelp">The same from the server, which is how to get back in when nobody can:
        <span class="mono">isemon --add-user name --role fulladmin</span> ·
        <span class="mono">isemon --passwd name</span> ·
        <span class="mono">isemon --users</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full" id="cols-card">
      <h2>Columns of the tables <span class="right" id="cols-count"></span></h2>
      <p class="note">Every table on these pages can be laid out the way you read it. Press
        your name at the top right and switch on <b>Edit the columns</b>; then drag the edge of
        a column's heading to make it wider or narrower, and drag the heading itself to move
        the column. Twice on an edge puts that table's widths back. Each change is kept with
        your account the moment you let go, not in this browser, and comes back wherever you
        sign in; nobody else's tables change. The switch is for this sign-in only: signing
        out, the sign-in running out or the service restarting turn it off again, and the
        columns stay as you left them.</p>
      <div class="btnrow" style="margin-top:12px">
        <button class="btn cmd" id="cols-reset" data-icon="undo"
                title="Every table back as it comes: the widths and the order of the columns you set, on every page. Nobody else's are touched.">Put my columns back as they come</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ---------- active directory ---------- -->
  <div class="ctl-pane" id="pane-dir" hidden>
    <div class="ctl-head">
      <h2 class="ctl-title">Active Directory</h2>
      <p class="ctl-lede">People sign in with the name and password they already have. A name
        that is <b>in</b> the directory is answered by the directory and by nothing else — the
        local account of the same name is not consulted, so an old local password cannot be a
        way in past a disabled domain account. A name that is <b>not</b> in it falls back to
        the local accounts, which is what keeps a way in on the morning the directory is
        down.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full"><div id="sect-dir"></div></div>

    <div class="card full">
      <h2>The certificate authority <span class="right" id="ca-when"></span></h2>
      <p class="note">Needed only when this reaches the directory over TLS and the certificate was signed by
      your own authority.</p>
      <div id="ca-state"></div>
      <textarea id="ca-pem" rows="6" class="mono admin-only" style="width:100%;margin-top:10px"
                placeholder="-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----&#10;MIID...&#10;-----END CERTIFICATE-----"></textarea>
      <div class="btnrow admin-only" style="margin-top:10px">
        <button class="btn cmd primary" id="ca-save" data-icon="upload"
                title="Read the certificate and keep it. It is checked for being a certificate at all before it is stored, and what it is gets shown above.">Import it</button>
        <button class="btn cmd" id="ca-file" data-icon="download"
