                title="Ask both directories about one name, exactly as the pages do, and show which of them answered.">Look a name up…</button>
      </div>
      <div id="dir2-out" style="margin-top:12px"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="card full">
      <h2>Its certificate authority <span class="right" id="ca2-when"></span></h2>
      <p class="note">Needed only when this reaches that directory over TLS and the
        certificate was signed by your own authority. It is kept apart from the first
        directory's, because the two domains are two domains.</p>
      <div id="ca2-state"></div>
      <textarea id="ca2-pem" rows="6" class="mono" style="width:100%;margin-top:10px"
                placeholder="-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----&#10;MIID...&#10;-----END CERTIFICATE-----"></textarea>
      <div class="btnrow admin-only" style="margin-top:10px">
        <button class="btn cmd primary" id="ca2-save" data-icon="upload"
                title="Read the certificate and keep it for the second directory.">Import it</button>
        <button class="btn cmd" id="ca2-file" data-icon="download"
                title="Pick a .crt or .pem file instead of pasting it.">Read a file…</button>
        <button class="btn cmd danger" id="ca2-drop" data-icon="trash"
                title="Take the stored authority away.">Take it away</button>
        <input type="file" id="ca2-input" accept=".pem,.crt,.cer,.txt" hidden>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- The reverse zone: the one thing that turns an address back into a
       machine somebody can name. A page of its own because the servers to
       ask are a list with an order, and because everything about how this
       behaves - that nothing waits for it, that an answer is written down
       with its time - belongs said in one place. -->
  <div class="ctl-pane" id="pane-names" hidden>
    <div class="ctl-head">
      <h2 class="ctl-title">Names of addresses</h2>
      <p class="ctl-lede">An address in a log is a fact about a machine nobody can read.
        The reverse zone turns <code>10.20.30.40</code> back into
        <code>nb-novak.example.loc</code> — over every address on these pages, in the popup
        about one, and in the box at the top, where a machine can then be found by the name
        people actually call it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full">
      <p class="note"><b>Nothing waits for any of it.</b> The asking happens on threads of
        its own: a page is drawn from the names already known, whatever is missing is asked
        for behind it and appears on the next draw, and a name server that has gone away
        leaves a name blank and changes nothing else. Not one lookup happens while a log is
        being read.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>Your servers, in your order — not this machine's resolver.</b> A
        site keeps more than one reverse zone and they are not all on the same server: the
        offices in the domain controllers, the DMZ somewhere else. They are asked in the
        order written here and the first one that hands over a <b>name</b> is the answer.
        One that does not answer, refuses, or says it has never heard of the address is
        passed over — because the next one along may be the one holding that zone.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>An answer is written down with its time.</b> A DHCP lease moves, so
        one address is one machine this morning and another this afternoon. A record from
        March is shown the name its address had in March, and the popup about an address
        lists every name it has had.</p>
      <div id="sect-names"></div>
      <div class="btnrow admin-only" style="margin-top:14px">
        <input type="search" id="ptr-try-ip" placeholder="10.20.30.40"
               style="max-width:220px"
               title="An address to look up now, of every server in the list, so that a server that has the zone and one that has never heard of it can be told apart.">
        <button class="btn cmd" id="ptr-try" data-icon="search"
                title="Ask every server in the list about this one address and show what each of them said. Every server is asked, rather than stopping at the first that answers, because what is being set up is the list itself.">Look one up…</button>
        <button class="btn cmd" id="ptr-fill" data-icon="history"
                title="Go back over every address already in the records and ask what it is called. Addresses are asked about as records arrive and as pages are drawn, so everything since this was switched on has an answer and nothing from before it does; this is the one pass that fills in the rest.">Fill in the ones with no name…</button>
        <span class="muted small" id="ptr-missing" style="align-self:center"></span>
      </div>
      <div id="ptr-out" style="margin-top:12px"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card full">
      <h2>How it is getting on <span class="right" id="ptr-state-when"></span></h2>
      <p class="note">What has been asked and what came back since this daemon started, and
        how many addresses have a name at all.</p>
      <div id="ptr-state"></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="ctl-pane" id="pane-db" hidden>
    <div class="ctl-head">
      <h2 class="ctl-title">The database</h2>
      <p class="ctl-lede">One database holds everything: the authentications read, who is
        banned, the story of every ban, the public lists, the country ranges and the accounts.
        It is a file on this machine unless you say otherwise.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full" id="db-engine-trouble" hidden></div>
    <div class="card full"><div id="sect-db"></div></div>
    <!-- The daily copy's own card, under the three settings that switch it on.
         What it keeps is not what the card lower down keeps: a dump is made by
         the engine's own program and goes back into that engine, a copy is made
         by this program and goes back into any of the three. -->
    <div class="card full" id="db-dump-card">
      <h2>The dumps it keeps <span class="right" id="db-dump-now"></span></h2>
      <p class="note">Every night at the time above, the database in use is dumped
        <b>by its own engine's own program</b> — <code>pg_dump</code> on PostgreSQL,
        <code>mariadb-dump</code> on MariaDB, and on the file SQLite's own backup, which is
        the file. It is a fraction of the size of the database, and it is the format the
        server's own tools read, so it can be put back by somebody who has never heard of
        this program.</p>
      <p class="note">A dump goes back into <b>the engine it came off</b>. What goes back
        into any of the three is a copy, which the card below makes — that is what carries an
        installation from the file to a server and back.</p>
      <div id="db-dump-says"></div>
      <!-- The dump being written, here where it was asked for. It used to be
           drawn only in the card above, off the screen of whoever pressed
           the button down here. -->
      <div class="going wide" id="db-dump-going" hidden>
        <div class="bar"><span></span></div>
        <div class="says"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="btnrow admin-only" style="margin-top:12px">
        <button class="btn cmd" id="db-dump-make" data-icon="save"
                title="Dump the database now, with the engine's own program, rather than waiting for tonight. It is kept with the others and counts as one of them.">Make one now</button>
        <button class="btn cmd" id="db-dump-get" data-icon="down"
                title="Dump the database now and save it to the machine you are sitting at. Nothing is kept here.">Download one</button>
      </div>
      <div id="db-dumps" style="margin-top:18px"></div>
      <div class="admin-only" id="db-dump-upload" style="margin-top:18px">
        <h4>Put a dump back</h4>
        <p class="note">A dump from somewhere else — one downloaded from here, or a file
          copied off the dumps folder. It replaces everything: every record, every ban, every
          setting and every account. Nothing happens until it has been read, checked and
          shown to you.</p>
        <div class="drop" id="dump-drop">
          <input type="file" id="dump-file"
                 accept=".pgc,.gz,.sql,.db,application/octet-stream" hidden>
          <span id="dump-drop-says">Drop a dump here, or press to choose one</span>
        </div>
        <div id="dump-upload-what" hidden></div>
        <div class="btnrow" style="margin-top:10px">
          <button class="btn cmd danger" id="dump-restore" data-icon="undo" disabled>Restore
            from this file</button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="card full" id="db-engine-card">
      <h2>Which one it is <span class="right" id="db-engine-now"></span></h2>
      <p class="note">The settings above say which database to open and how to reach it, and
        they are the only settings kept in <b id="db-engine-file">the config file</b> rather
        than in the database — a database cannot hold the address of itself, and a machine
        whose database will not open still has to be able to be put right from this page.</p>
      <p class="note">Changing the setting moves nothing. The order is: fill the address in,
        <b>try it</b>, <b>set it up</b> if the database and the account are not there yet,
        <b>carry everything across</b>, and only then <b>start using it</b>. Until that last
        step this installation carries on exactly as it is, and what is in it is never
        touched by any of this.</p>
      <div id="db-engine-says"></div>
      <div class="btnrow admin-only" style="margin-top:12px">
        <button class="btn cmd" id="db-engine-test" data-icon="check"
                title="Reach that server with the address above, exactly as the daemon would, and say what answered. Nothing is saved and nothing is written.">Try it</button>
        <button class="btn cmd" id="db-engine-setup" data-icon="key"
                title="Make the database and the account on that server, and give that account rights on that one database and no other, with a password made here and shown once. The first time it asks for an administrator on the server, uses it for this one connection and stores it nowhere. Afterwards it needs none: it opens the database and puts its tables right, which is worth doing after every upgrade.">Set it up…</button>
        <button class="btn cmd primary" id="db-engine-move" data-icon="save"
                title="Copy everything that is here into that database: every record, every ban, every setting and every account. What is here is left exactly as it is.">Carry everything across…</button>
        <button class="btn cmd danger" id="db-engine-empty" data-icon="forget"
                title="Throw away what is in the OTHER database, so that a move can be tried again. Never the one in use.">Empty it…</button>
        <button class="btn cmd" id="db-engine-use" data-icon="power"
                title="Start using it. This writes one setting and restarts the daemon; nothing in either database is touched, and coming back is the same four steps the other way.">Start using it…</button>
      </div>
      <p class="btnhelp">Once everything is in the other database and it is the one in use,
        the one it came from is just a spare copy: the file may be deleted, or the server
        uninstalled. Going the other way is these same steps with the file chosen instead —
        which is what makes it safe to try a server at all.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card full">
      <h2>The disk it is on</h2>
      <p class="note">How much room there is where this database's files live, how fast it is
        growing, and how long one will hold the other. Measured, not guessed: the size is
        noted once a day and what is shown is the slope of the last fortnight. On a database
        server the database is not on this machine at all — what is here then is the copies,
        and the line below says so.</p>
      <div id="db-disk"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card full" id="db-tune-card">
      <h2>The database server’s own settings</h2>
      <p class="note">A database server is installed with settings for a machine of any size,
        which is to say for a small one: PostgreSQL keeps 128 MB of the database in memory, and
        MariaDB likewise, on a machine of 32 GB. This reads what the machine the server runs on
        has — its memory, its processors, its disk — works the settings out for it, shows them
        beside what is there now, and puts them in when you say so.</p>
      <p class="note">Changing them takes an account that may: this program’s own where it has
        that right, otherwise an administrator on that server, asked for in the popup, used for
        that one request and stored nowhere — not in the settings, not in the database, not in
        any log.</p>
      <div id="db-tune-says"></div>
      <div class="btnrow admin-only" style="margin-top:12px">
        <button class="btn cmd primary" id="db-tune" data-icon="gear"
                title="Read what the machine the database server runs on has, work its settings out for it and show them beside what is there now. Nothing is changed until you say so.">Tune it for this machine…</button>
        <button class="btn cmd" id="db-tune-restart" data-icon="power" hidden
                title="Restart PostgreSQL for the settings that take effect only at its start.">Restart the server…</button>
        <button class="btn cmd" id="db-tune-undo" data-icon="undo" hidden
                title="Put every setting this page changed back to what it was before.">Put back as it was…</button>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="card full">
      <h2>What is in it <span class="right" id="db-size"></span></h2>
      <p class="note">A copy is consistent even while the daemon is writing to it, and it is
        written as a portable file whatever this is running on — so a copy made on MariaDB or
        PostgreSQL goes back into either of those, or into the file on this machine.</p>
      <p class="btnhelp">These are <b>every row in each table</b>, which is not the number on
        the tabs above: those count what the page they belong to is showing — a window of
        time, and the doors that page is about. The eduroam and token records are in here and
        are not on the Failed attempts tabs, which is most of the difference on a site that
        reads them.</p>
      <div id="db-counts"></div>
      <!-- The modules whose records live inside those two tables rather than
           in one of their own. A site that has just switched one on asks
           how much of the database it is now, and a card counting tables
           cannot answer it: the whole of a tac_plus history reads as
           "successful logins". -->
      <div id="db-inside" style="margin-top:18px"></div>
      <!-- Whatever the database is busy with, while it is busy with it. A
           copy of a gigabyte is minutes of work, and a button that spins
           for five minutes saying nothing is a button people press again. -->
      <div class="going wide" id="db-going" hidden>
        <div class="bar"><span id="db-going-fill"></span></div>
        <div class="says" id="db-going-says"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="btnrow" style="margin-top:12px">
        <button class="btn cmd admin-only" id="db-backup" data-icon="save"
                title="Copy the database as it is into the backups folder on this machine, now, rather than waiting for tonight.">Make a copy now</button>
        <button class="btn cmd admin-only" id="db-download" data-icon="down">Download it</button>
        <button class="btn cmd admin-only" id="db-download-full" data-icon="down">Download a full backup</button>
        <button class="btn cmd admin-only" id="db-check" data-icon="check"
                title="Ask the database to read itself through and say whether anything in it is broken - SQLite&#39;s integrity check, MariaDB&#39;s CHECK TABLE, and on PostgreSQL every row of every table read through, with every index checked as well where the server has amcheck. It only reads; on a large one it takes a while.">Check it</button>
<!-- Three buttons that all sound like tidying, so each one says which
             of the three it is. In order: throw away what the settings
             already say is too old; throw away authentications older than an
             age chosen here and now; and give the disk back afterwards.
             Nothing gives the disk back on its own - deleting rows frees
             room INSIDE the file on all three engines, and only a rebuild
             hands it to the operating system. -->
        <button class="btn cmd admin-only" id="db-tidy" data-icon="broom"
                title="Throws away exactly what the &quot;keep for&quot; numbers under Settings already say is too old — the refused attempts, the sign-ins, this program&#39;s own event log and ban history, and the domain events, each by its own number of days. It happens by itself as the rows age (or once a day, if that is set); this is the same thing, now. Nothing is chosen here and nothing inside the keeping is touched. Lowering one of those numbers deletes nothing by itself: this is what does it.">Apply what is kept, now</button>
        <button class="btn cmd danger admin-only" id="db-prune" data-icon="forget"
                title="Forgets recorded authentications — the refused attempts and the sign-ins — older than an age you choose here, whatever the &quot;keep for&quot; numbers say. For clearing out a year in one go rather than waiting for the retention to do it. Only those two kinds of record: who is banned, the story of every ban, the domain events and the settings are not touched.">Forget records older than…</button>
        <button class="btn cmd admin-only" id="db-optimize" data-icon="save"
                title="Gives the space back to the DISK. Deleting rows frees room inside the database file and the engine reuses it; nothing hands it back to the operating system until the whole thing is rebuilt. All three do that the same way — SQLite&#39;s VACUUM, MariaDB&#39;s OPTIMIZE TABLE, PostgreSQL&#39;s VACUUM FULL — and all three hold the table shut while they do it and need room for a second copy of the biggest one. Worth doing after throwing a lot away, and not otherwise.">Give the disk back</button>
      </div>
      <div id="db-backups" style="margin-top:18px"></div>
      <div class="admin-only" id="db-upload" style="margin-top:18px">
        <h4>Put a copy back</h4>
        <p class="note">A database from somewhere else — one downloaded from here, or a file
          copied off the backups folder. It replaces everything: every record, every ban,
          every setting and every account. Nothing happens until it has been read, checked
          and shown to you.</p>
        <div class="drop" id="db-drop">
          <input type="file" id="db-file" accept=".db,.sqlite,.sqlite3,application/octet-stream"
                 hidden>
          <span id="db-drop-says">Drop a database file here, or press to choose one</span>
        </div>
        <div id="db-upload-what" hidden></div>
        <div class="btnrow" style="margin-top:10px">
          <button class="btn cmd danger" id="db-restore" data-icon="undo" disabled>Restore from
            this file</button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card full">
      <h2>Erase the collected data <span class="right" id="erase-size"></span></h2>
      <p class="note">Everything here can be got again — read from the logs a second time,
        fetched from the internet, or worked out afresh — which is what makes it erasable.
        Choose what goes; nothing is erased that you have not ticked.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>Your accounts, every setting and the whitelist are never touched by
        this.</b> They are not options you could tick by mistake: they are not in the list at
        all. Neither is the ssh key for the router.</p>
      <div id="erase-list" class="erase-list"></div>
      <div class="btnrow admin-only" style="margin-top:14px">
        <button class="btn cmd danger" id="erase-go" data-icon="forget"
                title="Erase everything ticked above. It cannot be undone — but a copy of the database made just before it can be restored, and the button offers to make one.">Erase what is ticked…</button>
        <label class="check small" id="erase-tidy-wrap">
          <input type="checkbox" id="erase-tidy" checked
                 title="Tidy the database afterwards so the space actually goes back to the disk. None of the three give room back by themselves when rows are deleted — it is reused inside instead. It takes as long again as the erasing did and needs as much free space as the database is big.">
          <span>rebuild the file afterwards, to give the disk back</span>
        </label>
      </div>
      <div class="btnrow admin-only" style="margin-top:14px">
        <button class="btn cmd danger" id="past-forget" data-icon="forget">Erase the ban history…</button>
      </div>
      <p class="btnhelp">Only the story of the bans: when each started, what earned it and when
        it ended. Whoever is banned now stays banned, and nothing else in the database is
        touched &mdash; but the ladder counts this history, so everybody starts again at the
        bottom step afterwards.</p>
      <p class="btnhelp">Erasing the ISE records does <b>not</b> make those logs be read
        again: the daemon carries on from where it is in each file. To read them from the
        beginning and apply what they earn, use <i>Go back over every log</i> under Banning.
        The token and eduroam records are the exception — erasing either of those also forgets
        where its files were read up to, so what is still in them is read again from the
        beginning, with the clock as it is now.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

