    + 'already names goes on the first time it fails here at all — it is on somebody '
    + 'else’s list of known-bad <b>and</b> it has now knocked on this door, which is two '
    + 'things rather than one. The third group is whatever somebody put there by hand.<br><br>'
    + 'Anything under <i>never kept out</i> is left alone whatever it does, and how long a ban '
    + 'lasts is in Settings.',
  'counting up to a ban': 'The addresses the counting can see right now — inside the '
    + 'window and not yet out — and how far each one still has to go.<br><br>It is the '
    + 'half of the story this page never showed: an empty list above says nothing about '
    + 'whether nothing is happening or whether everything is happening one attempt below the '
    + 'line.<br><br>An address is counted from the end of its last ban: what it was banned '
    + 'for once is not charged to it again, so serving a ban and being let back in both start '
    + 'it again at nought.<br><br>Only attempts at the VPN are counted here. A machine failing '
    + '802.1X or MAB on a socket, or somebody mistyping a password on a switch over TACACS+, is '
    + 'recorded and shown on its own tab and can never earn anybody a ban — the address on such '
    + 'a record is usually the switch itself.',
  'ban history': 'Every ban there has ever been, kept long after the address was let back in. '
    + 'It is what the ladder counts, and what somebody reads when they want to know why an '
    + 'address they have never heard of was on the router last Tuesday.',
  'failed attempts': 'Every authentication the two ISE nodes refused, newest first. Press a '
    + 'row for the whole record — every field ISE wrote, exactly as it wrote it.',
  'the firewalls handed down': 'Every downloadable ACL the VPN logins were given, how '
    + 'many logins each one, and whether its rules are among the records kept '
    + 'here.<br><br>A device downloads an ACL only when it does not already hold it, and the '
    + 'name carries ISE\u2019s own hash of the contents \u2014 so the one your busiest group '
    + 'uses all day may have been fetched once, months ago, and never since, while a rarely '
    + 'used one is fetched afresh every few hours as it falls out of the device\u2019s '
    + 'cache. The logins name it either way.<br><br>That is why a login can say which '
    + 'firewall it was handed and still show no rules: nothing is missing here, the download '
    + 'simply happened before these logs. It appears the next time the device has to fetch '
    + 'it \u2014 after a reload, after the cache drops it, or as soon as the ACL is edited '
    + 'on ISE, which changes its name and forces a fresh download.',
  'successful logins': 'Everybody ISE let in, newest first.<br><br>Click a row for the '
    + 'whole record: on a VPN login the authorisation profile, the directory groups, the '
    + 'policy that matched, and the firewall the session was handed \u2014 the downloadable '
    + 'ACL, drawn as rules rather than as one long line. That ACL arrives as a record of its '
    + 'own in the same log; it is not a login, so it is not listed as one.<br><br>On the '
    + 'TACACS side ISE writes two kinds: <b>Authentication</b> is somebody signing in, '
    + '<b>Authorization</b> is a command they were allowed to run once in. The commands are '
    + 'shown inside the session they were typed in rather than beside it.',
  'what the firewall is carrying': 'The list on the firewall is the truth about what is '
    + 'being stopped; the reason, the story and the attempts that earned it live here. Neither '
    + 'half means much alone, so both are in one place.<br><br>Only entries carrying this '
    + 'daemon\u2019s mark are ever added or removed \u2014 anything you put on that list by '
    + 'hand is left exactly as it is, and every other address list on it is never '
    + 'mentioned.<br><br>Where there is a pair, both are read and both are written, and this '
    + 'page says whether the two are carrying the same thing.',
  'what this daemon has done': 'Every ban, every change of a setting, every sign-in and every '
    + 'conversation with the router. Kept in the database, so it survives a restart.',
  'the disk it is on': 'How much room there is where the database lives, how fast the file is '
    + 'actually growing — measured once a day, not guessed — and how long one will '
    + 'hold the other.',
  'its certificate authority': 'Needed when the <b>second</b> directory is reached over TLS '
    + 'and its certificate was signed by your own authority. It is kept apart from the first '
    + 'directory\u2019s: two domains are two domains, and one of them being replaced should '
    + 'not take the other\u2019s trust with it.',
  'the certificate authority': 'Needed when the directory is reached over TLS and its '
    + 'certificate was signed by your own authority rather than by a public one \u2014 which '
    + 'on a domain controller it almost always was.<br><br>Paste the authority\u2019s '
    + 'certificate here, in PEM form, and the server\u2019s certificate can be checked '
    + 'properly instead of the check being switched off.',
  'go back over every log': 'Normally a record is weighed as it arrives, and one older '
    + 'than a day is written down but not acted on \u2014 which is what stops a machine '
    + 'installed on a Tuesday from banning half the internet for what it did in '
    + 'June.<br><br>This does the opposite, once, because you asked: every log is read from '
    + 'the beginning \u2014 the rotated copies beside it as well, so the days before this '
    + 'daemon was installed are read too \u2014 and everything in them is weighed by the '
    + 'rules as they stand now.<br><br>Nothing is stored twice: every record is kept under '
    + 'the session id ISE gave it, so one already here is recognised and left exactly as it '
    + 'is and only what was missing is added. The pass says both numbers when it finishes, so '
    + 'there is nothing to take on trust.',
  'never banned': 'Addresses nothing may ever ban, whatever they do. Your own offices, your '
    + 'own monitoring, the address you are reading this from. It beats every rule here, '
    + 'including a ban somebody makes by hand.',
  'lists somebody else keeps': 'Public blacklists, fetched on a timer. Nothing is banned for '
    + 'being on one alone \u2014 most of those addresses will never come near this VPN \u2014 '
    + 'but the first one that does fail here goes on the list for good.',
  'what is fetched, and when it is next due': 'Everything this downloads and when each is due '
    + 'again. A machine with no way out simply never fetches and says so, rather than '
    + 'failing quietly.',
  'fold now': 'Replace separately banned addresses with the subnet that holds them. A scanner '
    + 'renting a /24 and working through it is forty rules doing the work of one, and the '
    + 'fortieth address is banned an hour after the first.',
  'the mikrotik': 'How this signs in to the router, and what it may touch there. It is given '
    + 'one address list and never looks at another; your firewall rules, filters and routes '
    + 'are never read or written.',
  'the country database': 'The free monthly list of which ranges belong to which country. It '
    + 'is what every flag on this page comes from, and what the country list on the router '
    + 'is built out of.',
  'the country list on the router': 'A second address list on the same firewall, holding '
    + 'whole countries rather than individual offenders.<br><br>Nothing of the banning touches '
    + 'it and it touches nothing of the banning: what the firewall does with it \u2014 '
    + 'accept, drop, mangle, route \u2014 is a rule you write there, pointed at this '
    + 'list\u2019s name.<br><br>Switch it on above, choose the countries, and it is refilled '
    + 'by itself whenever the country database is refreshed.',
  'who may open this': 'The accounts that may open this dashboard.<br><br>Whoever is '
    + 'signed in can always change their own password; nobody but a full admin can change '
    + 'anybody else\u2019s.<br><br>An account marked <b>from AD</b> is the directory\u2019s: '
    + 'its password lives in the domain and what it may do comes from its groups there, '
    + 'worked out again every time it signs in \u2014 neither can be set here. Switching it '
    + 'off is local, and stops it getting in whatever the domain says.',
  'who has signed in from the directory': 'Written down as people sign in, so that a session, '
    + 'an event and a \u201cbanned by\u201d have a name to point at. There is no password '
    + 'here for any of them.',
  'what is in it': 'How many rows of each kind the database holds. It is all one database: the '
    + 'authentications read, who is banned, the story of every ban, the public lists, the '
    + 'country ranges and the accounts.',
  'erase the collected data': 'Empties what can be got again \u2014 the records read from the '
    + 'logs, the fetched lists, the country database \u2014 and never the accounts, the '
    + 'settings or the whitelist, which are not even offered here.',
  'carrying this somewhere else': 'The whole configuration as one JSON file, to rebuild '
    + 'this machine somewhere else or to keep beside a backup.<br><br>There are two shapes of '
    + 'it. The <b>plain</b> one is safe to mail and to paste into a ticket: it has no '
    + 'passwords, no ssh key and no certificate in it, and a machine built from it comes up '
    + 'unable to reach the firewall or ask the domain anything until those are typed '
    + 'in.<br><br>The <b>complete</b> one has all of them, so a machine built from it needs '
    + 'nothing typed in at all \u2014 and is a file that gets into your firewall, so keep it '
    + 'the way you keep the keys themselves.',
  'the groups, and what each one may do': 'Pick the groups out of the directory rather than '
    + 'typing a distinguished name by hand — that is how a role ends up pointing at a '
    + 'group that does not exist, and nobody finds out until the first person cannot sign in.'
};

/** The words of a heading, without the count or the select beside it. */
function headingWords(node) {
  var text = '';
  Array.prototype.forEach.call(node.childNodes, function (bit) {
    if (bit.nodeType === 3) text += srcText(bit);      /* in English, whatever is shown */
  });
  return text.trim().toLowerCase().replace(/\s+/g, ' ');
}

/* The little marks a page is read by, by the words on them. */
var TAG_SAYS = {
  'read from the log': 'A download of this ACL is among the records kept here, so the rules '
    + 'it holds can be shown under the logins that were handed it.',
  'not downloaded here': 'No device has fetched this ACL while these logs have been read, so '
    + 'its rules cannot be shown. A device fetches an ACL only when it does not already hold '
    + 'it — this one it has held throughout. It appears after a reload of the device, '
    + 'after its cache drops it, or as soon as the ACL is edited on ISE.',
  'both carrying the same': 'The two firewalls hold exactly the same addresses of ours, which '
    + 'is what a pair is for: whichever of them takes the traffic is already dropping '
    + 'everything the other is.',
  'one of them could not be read': 'One of the pair did not answer, so the two cannot be '
    + 'compared at all. What is shown for each of them is what it last said. An outage is not '
    + 'the same as a firewall carrying nothing.',
  'could not be read': 'This firewall did not answer when the page was drawn. Nothing is known '
    + 'about what it is carrying right now — which is different from knowing it is empty. The '
    + 'words behind this are whatever ssh said.',
  'answering': 'This firewall answered when the page was drawn, so the numbers beside it were '
    + 'read from it rather than remembered.',
  'none': 'This firewall is carrying every address this machine says it should be. Nothing is '
    + 'waiting to go on it.',
  'in place': 'The country database has been fetched and is on this machine. Every address on '
    + 'every page can be given a country, and a country list can be put on the router. It does '
    + 'not go stale in any way that matters: ranges move between countries slowly.',
  'ready': 'Everything this needs has been set and it can run now. What is missing is said '
    + 'here instead when it is not.',
  'trouble': 'The last attempt did not work, and what went wrong is written beside this. '
    + 'Nothing has been lost — whatever could not be done is still to be done and will be '
    + 'tried again — but until this clears, what is on the router is not what is on this '
    + 'page.',
  'this list': 'This line was added on this page, rather than coming from the '
    + '<code>never_block</code> setting. Both mean the same thing and neither wins over the '
    + 'other.',
  'current': 'This was fetched recently enough to count as up to date. How recent that has '
    + 'to be is the interval beside it.',
  'geoip': 'This one is the country database rather than a blacklist: what it decides is '
    + 'which flag an address gets, and what the country list on the router holds.',
  'not set up': 'No router has been given an address yet, so nothing is being carried '
    + 'anywhere. Everything is still recorded and the list is still kept here.',
  'not fetched': 'This has never been downloaded, so there is nothing to work from yet.',
  'switched off': 'This is turned off, so nothing here does anything. What was collected is '
    + 'kept either way.',
  'you': 'This is the account you are signed in with. It is the one account you cannot remove '
    + 'or switch off from here — locking yourself out would need somebody at the server.',
  'still on': 'This ban has not ended: either it is for good, or its clock has not run out.',
  'for good': 'There is no clock on this one. It stays until somebody takes it off.',
  'refusals': 'This file holds the authentications ISE refused. It is what the banning is '
    + 'made of.',
  'logins': 'This file holds the authentications ISE let through — the VPN logins and '
    + 'the TACACS sign-ins to switches.',
  'not there': 'This file does not exist. On a pair where one node has been quiet that is '
    + 'ordinary; it only matters when no node has one.',
  'in sync': 'The firewall is carrying what this says it should be carrying. On a pair, '
    + 'both of them are.',
  'out of sync': 'The firewall is not carrying what this says it should be. The numbers '
    + 'underneath say by how much, and “Put the lists on it” settles it.',
  'cannot be told': 'The firewall would not answer the question, which is not the same as '
    + 'the answer being nothing.',
  'elsewhere': 'This sign-in did not come from one of the addresses under <b>Where TACACS '
    + 'sign-ins come from</b>. Nothing was blocked — it is the row to read first.',
  'up to date': 'Everything written to this file has been read.',
  'must change': 'This account has to choose a new password the next time it signs in.',
  'full admin': 'This account may change anything: ban, unban, settings, the router, other '
    + 'accounts.',
  'read only': 'This account may look at every page and change nothing \u2014 no ban, no '
    + 'setting, not even a fetch.',
  'on': 'This account may sign in.',
  'off': 'This account cannot sign in. Everything about it is kept and it can be switched '
    + 'back on.',
  'already empty': 'There is nothing here to erase.'
};

/** Put the long words behind every short name that has some. */
function explainTheNames(where) {
  var host = where || document;
  if (host.querySelectorAll === undefined) return;
  Array.prototype.forEach.call(host.querySelectorAll('th'), function (th) {
    if (th.dataset.explains || th.getAttribute('title')) return;
    /* The heading's own words: the sort arrow and the grip drawn into it are
       not part of its name, and with them in it most headings found no help
       at all. (1.10.160) */
    var said = COLUMN_SAYS[(th.dataset.words || colsLabel(th) || srcText(th) || '')
      .replace(/[⇅▲▼]/g, '').trim().toLowerCase()];
    if (said) explain(th, said);
  });
  Array.prototype.forEach.call(host.querySelectorAll('.card > h2, .ctl-pane h2'),
    function (h2) {
      if (h2.dataset.explains || h2.getAttribute('title')) return;
      var said = HEADING_SAYS[headingWords(h2)];
      if (said) explain(h2, said);
    });
  Array.prototype.forEach.call(host.querySelectorAll('.tag'), function (tag) {
    if (tag.dataset.explains || tag.getAttribute('title')) return;
    var said = TAG_SAYS[(srcText(tag) || '').trim().toLowerCase()];
    if (said) explain(tag, said);
  });
}

/* Anything drawn anywhere, at any time, gets the same treatment. Half these
   tables are built in five different places and a new one would otherwise
   arrive with nothing to say about itself - which is how a page ends up
   explained in patches. */
var NAMING = null;
var NAMING_ROOTS = [];
if (window.MutationObserver) {
  var namingSoon = null;
  NAMING = new MutationObserver(function (records) {
    /* Not for the clock, a countdown or our own panel being redrawn: a
       single ban's clock ticking once a second had the whole page's
       headings, tags and columns walked once a second for ever. */
    var worth = records.some(function (m) {
      var t = m.target && m.target.nodeType === 1 ? m.target : (m.target && m.target.parentNode);
      if (!t || !t.closest) return true;
      return !t.closest('#updated-at, #state-pill, .sethelp, .tip, [data-until]');
    });
    if (!worth) return;
    /* Only where something was drawn (1.10.168): the whole document was
       walked after every change, hidden pages and all, and it grew with
       every tab somebody had opened - sixty milliseconds a sweep, a dozen
       sweeps a minute, on a page doing nothing. */
    records.forEach(function (m) {
      var t = m.target && m.target.nodeType === 1 ? m.target : (m.target && m.target.parentNode);
      if (t && t.nodeType === 1) NAMING_ROOTS.push(t);
    });
    clearTimeout(namingSoon);
    /* The whole sweep, not only the names: whatever was just drawn is
       explained and folded now, once, rather than by the next poll's walk
       of the page - which is how the polls that bring nothing new can
       leave the page alone. */
    namingSoon = setTimeout(function () {
      var roots = NAMING_ROOTS;
      NAMING_ROOTS = [];
      if (roots.length > 400) { explainWhatWasDrawn(document); return; }
      var keep = roots.filter(function (r, i) {
        if (!document.contains(r) || roots.indexOf(r) !== i) return false;
        return !roots.some(function (o) { return o !== r && o.contains(r); });
      });
      keep.forEach(function (r) { explainWhatWasDrawn(r.parentNode || r); });
    }, 120);
  });
  NAMING.observe(document.body, {childList: true, subtree: true});
}

function explainWhatWasDrawn(where) {
  var host = where || document;
  Array.prototype.forEach.call(host.querySelectorAll('[title]'), function (one) {
    if (one.dataset.explains) {
      /* Already saying something of its own, and something has put a
         browser tooltip back on it. That happens on every poll: the header's
         health pill and the clock beside it are given a fresh `title` each
         time they are redrawn, long after this ran, and from then on there
         are two - ours where the pointer is and the browser's pale one under
         it a second later, saying the same thing in a different place.
         The words are not thrown away with the attribute: they are what it
         says NOW, so they become what our own panel says. */
      var said = one.getAttribute('title');
      one.removeAttribute('title');
      if (said && one.dataset.explains === '1') {
        one._explainHtml = '<div>' + richText(said) + '</div>';
      }
      return;
    }
    if (one.closest && one.closest('.sethelp')) return;
    explain(one, one.getAttribute('title'));
  });
  explainTheNames(host);
  nameTheAddresses(host);
  nameTheMakers(host);
  foldNested(host);
  if (typeof markSortableTables === 'function') markSortableTables(host);
}

/* Anything left explaining itself inside something else that does, folded in.
   `explain` already does this in both directions as it goes - a line joining
   the panel it is part of, a panel taking in the lines already inside it -
   and there is one case neither can catch: an element explained BEFORE it is
   put on the page at all. Built, given its words, then appended into a row
   that was made pressable a moment earlier, it has no parent to look up
   through at the time and the row has nothing to look down at. Two boxes,
   and which one somebody sees comes down to where the pointer crossed.

   So the page is swept once after each redraw, when everything is where it
   finally is. One thing pointed at is one panel, with all of it in. */
function foldNested(where) {
  /* Nothing is folded any more (1.10.156): see `explain`. Kept so that the
     sweep after a redraw has one name to call. */
  return where;
}
/* Where the pointer actually is, so that a panel is never taken away from
   under somebody who has not moved. */
var MOUSE_AT = {x: -1, y: -1};
document.addEventListener('mousemove', function (e) {
  MOUSE_AT = {x: e.clientX, y: e.clientY};
}, true);

/* A panel left open over something that has since been redrawn away points at
   nothing - but only once somebody has MOVED. A list that redraws itself
   every few seconds used to take the row out from under a still pointer, and
   this closed the panel a second later: a popup that vanishes by itself
   while it is being read. Nothing redraws under a pointer any more (see
   `pointerBusy`); this is what catches the rest. */
setInterval(function () {
  if (!SETHELP || SETHELP.style.display !== 'block') return;
  var over = SETHELP._over;
  if (!over || over.isConnected) return;
  var was = SETHELP._mouseAt;
  if (was && Math.abs(was.x - MOUSE_AT.x) < 4 && Math.abs(was.y - MOUSE_AT.y) < 4) {
    return;                      // the pointer has not moved; leave it be
  }
  hideHelp();
}, 1000);
window.addEventListener('scroll', hideHelp, true);

/* ---- tabs ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
/** The address bar says which page is open, so reloading lands on it again.

    One place writes it, out of both halves at once. It used to be written by
    whichever of the two ran last, and on every load that was the settings
    rail - so the address said `#settings/logs` while the overview was on the
    screen, and pressing F5 opened settings. */
function writeHash() {
  var want = '#' + (TAB || 'overview');
  if (TAB === 'settings') want += '/' + (PANE || 'logs');
  /* ...and which page or door of the tab, when it is not the one the tab
     opens on, so a link or F5 lands where somebody was. (1.10.160) */
  var sub = hashSub(TAB);
  if (sub) want += '/' + sub;
  if (location.hash === want) return;
  try { history.replaceState(null, '', want); } catch (e) { /* a file:// page */ }
}

function hashSub(tab) {
  if (tab === 'traffic' && typeof TPAGE !== 'undefined' && TPAGE && TPAGE !== 'over') return TPAGE;
  if (tab === 'windows' && typeof WPAGE_SHOWN !== 'undefined' && WPAGE_SHOWN === 'events') {
    return 'events';
  }
  if (tab === 'failed' && typeof FKIND !== 'undefined' && FKIND && FKIND !== 'vpn') return FKIND;
  if (tab === 'logins' && typeof PKIND !== 'undefined' && PKIND && PKIND !== 'vpn') return PKIND;
  return '';
}

/** The page or door an address names, opened once its tab is up. Anything
    it does not know is left alone: the tab opens as it always has. */
function openHashSub(tab, sub) {
  if (!sub || sub === hashSub(tab)) return;
  if (tab === 'traffic') {
    var b = document.querySelector('#t-pages button[data-page="' + sub.replace(/[^a-z0-9_-]/gi, '') + '"]');
    /* A page's button is hidden until the first counts say it has lines -
       which on a fresh load is after this runs, so a link to the threats or
       to the VPN opened the overview instead. A page that exists is opened;
       showing it shows its button. (1.10.168) */
    if (b && (!b.hidden || T_PAGES[sub] || sub === 'vpn' || sub === 'router')) tShowPage(sub);
  } else if (tab === 'windows') {
    if (sub === 'events') winShowPage('events');
  } else if (tab === 'failed') {
    if (DOOR_SAYS[sub]) showFailedKind(sub);
  } else if (tab === 'logins') {
    if (DOOR_SAYS[sub]) passedTab(sub);
  }
}

/** Which long cards may take the whole window, and which may not.

    A card whose rows scroll inside it is the right shape for a list of a
    hundred thousand events: the search box and the pager stay where somebody
    left them. It is the WRONG shape when something else is on the page
    underneath it - the logins have the access lists below them - because a
    card capped to the window puts whatever is under it below the fold for
    ever, and gives the page a second scrollbar to reach it with.

    So the markup says which cards are long (`data-hold`) and this says which
    of them may hold: only where the card is the only one on its page. Run
    once and again whenever a page is shown, because a card can be hidden and
    shown and the answer changes with it.
*/
function holdTheTallOnes() {
  /* Whatever was a column last time is not necessarily one now: a different
     page is being shown, or a second card has appeared beside the first. */
  Array.prototype.forEach.call(document.querySelectorAll('.holds-one'),
    function (one) { one.classList.remove('holds-one'); });
  Array.prototype.forEach.call(document.querySelectorAll('[data-hold]'), function (card) {
    var panel = card.closest ? card.closest('.panel') : null;
    var alone = true;
    if (panel) {
      var cards = panel.querySelectorAll('.card.full');
      alone = Array.prototype.filter.call(cards, function (one) {
        /* A card inside the one being measured is part of it, not a
           neighbour; a hidden page's cards are not on this page; and a card
           only an administrator can see is not there for everybody else. */
        return one !== card && !card.contains(one) && !isTuckedAway(one)
               && !(one.classList.contains('admin-only') && !ADMIN)
               && !(one.classList.contains('ban-only') && !CAN_BAN);
      }).length === 0;
    }
    card.classList.toggle('held', alone);
    card.style.maxHeight = '';
    if (!alone || isTuckedAway(card)) return;
    /* Every step from the panel down to the card becomes a column, so the
       card grows into whatever room the window has left it. Nothing is
       measured: measuring how far the content overran was right to within a
       pixel or two, and a pixel or two is a scrollbar down the whole height
       of the window, which is the thing this is here to make impossible. */
    for (var up = card.parentNode; up && up !== panel; up = up.parentNode) {
      if (up.classList) up.classList.add('holds-one');
    }
    if (panel) panel.classList.add('holds-one');
    fitTheNotes(card);
  });
}

/** A paragraph of explanation, cut to two lines with the rest one press away.

    Three lines of note above a table is three rows of data nobody can see.
    Nothing is hidden: the whole of it is still there, the press is the
    paragraph itself, and a note short enough to fit is left alone. */
function fitTheNotes(card) {
  Array.prototype.forEach.call(card.children, function (note) {
    if (!note.classList || !note.classList.contains('note')) return;
    if (note.dataset.cuts) return;
    note.dataset.cuts = '1';
    note.onclick = function () {
      note.classList.toggle('open');
      note.classList.toggle('cut', !note.classList.contains('open')
                            && note.scrollHeight > note.clientHeight + 2);
      holdTheTallOnes();
    };
    note.classList.toggle('cut', note.scrollHeight > note.clientHeight + 2);
    note.title = '';
  });
}

/* The room a card has changes with the window, and so does whether a note
   still needs cutting. */
window.addEventListener('resize', function () {
  clearTimeout(window._heldSoon);
  window._heldSoon = setTimeout(holdTheTallOnes, 120);
});

/** Whether something is inside a part of the page that is not being shown. */
function isTuckedAway(node) {
  for (var up = node; up && up.nodeType === 1; up = up.parentNode) {
    if (up.hidden) return true;
  }
  return false;
}

function showTab(name) {
  /* Another tab: every popup goes, none comes back over it (1.10.241). */
  if (!$('modal').hidden) closeAllModals();
  TAB = name;
  Array.prototype.forEach.call(document.querySelectorAll('.tabs button[data-tab]'),
    function (b) {
      b.setAttribute('aria-selected', b.getAttribute('data-tab') === name ? 'true' : 'false');
    });
  ['overview', 'bans', 'failed', 'logins', 'traffic', 'windows', 'router', 'events',
   'logs', 'settings'].forEach(
    function (one) {
      var panel = $('tab-' + one);
      if (panel) panel.hidden = one !== name;
    });
  writeHash();
  /* Which of the long cards may take the whole window, now that a different
     page is the one being shown. */
  holdTheTallOnes();
  onTab(name);
}
Array.prototype.forEach.call(document.querySelectorAll('.tabs button[data-tab]'), function (b) {
  b.onclick = function () { showTab(b.getAttribute('data-tab')); };
});

function onTab(name) {
  /* Whatever this tab holds, swept the moment it is shown rather than on the
     next poll five seconds later. Most of it is in the page from the start
     and carries a `title` written into the markup; until something sweeps it,
     that title is the browser's own pale box and ours is beside it. Five
     seconds is long enough for somebody to point at a filter and see both. */
  explainWhatWasDrawn(document);
  if (name === 'overview') loadDashboard();
  if (name === 'bans') { loadBans(); loadPast(); }
  if (name === 'failed') loadFailed();
  if (name === 'logins') { loadPassed(); if (PKIND === 'vpn') loadAcls(); }
  if (name === 'router') { if (!$('router-out').firstChild) readRouter(); }
  if (name === 'windows') loadWindows();
  if (name === 'traffic') { loadTraffic(); if (typeof tFitSoon === 'function') tFitSoon(); }
  if (name === 'events') loadEvents();
  if (name === 'settings') loadSettings();
  /* Drawn from the status every poll draws the header from, so it is there
     already; drawn again at once so a page opened straight onto it does not
     wait for the next poll. */
  if (name === 'logs' && STATUS) paintHeader(STATUS);
}

/* ---- live updates ---------------------------------------------------------
   One poll, and the rest follows from it. The daemon keeps a number that goes
   up whenever anything changes, so a page that is only watching costs one
   small answer every few seconds and redraws nothing until there is something
   to redraw. */
var TICK_BUSY = false;
var TICK_HIDDEN = 0;
var TICK_SEQ = 0;
var WIN_TICK_AT = 0, SERIAL_FW, FW_MOVED_AT = 0;
function tick() {
  /* The ban cells of tables drawn over since, let go (1.10.198): every
     redraw added its cells and only a change of a ban took the old ones
     away, so a page left open all day held every table it ever drew. Here,
     between tasks, nothing is half-built. */
  if (BANCELLS.length) {
    BANCELLS = BANCELLS.filter(function (one) { return one.tds[0].isConnected; });
  }
  if (!LIVE) return;
  /* A tab nobody is looking at asks once a minute rather than every five
     seconds: a dashboard left open in a background tab all day was twelve
     status questions a minute, and each moved number set the page it was
     on to asking again. The moment it is looked at, it asks at once
     (below). */
  if (document.hidden) {
    if (Date.now() - TICK_HIDDEN < 60000) return;
    TICK_HIDDEN = Date.now();
  }
  /* One status question in flight at a time. On a server slow enough that
     one takes longer than the five seconds between them, they piled up -
     each one slower for the ones beside it - and an older answer landing
     after a newer one redrew the page backwards. A tick that finds one
     still out simply lets it be; the next tick is five seconds away. */
  /* Until the first answer, a question is waited on fifteen seconds, not a
     minute (1.10.243): the page opened while the daemon was still starting
     stayed empty for that minute. */
  if (TICK_BUSY && Date.now() - TICK_BUSY < (LAST_OK ? 65000 : 15000)) return;
  /* ...but never for ever: a question the browser is still waiting on after
     half a minute is one the relay has already given up on, and holding the
     flag for it would stop the page asking again. The flag is the moment it
     was set, so age is what is checked. */
  TICK_BUSY = Date.now();
  /* Numbered, so that an answer to an older question landing after a newer
     one's is dropped rather than drawn: the relay waits a minute for a read,
     and the guard above gave up at half of that, so two could be out at
     once and the page went backwards. (1.10.175) */
  var mine = ++TICK_SEQ;
  /* The files being read only where they are drawn. (1.10.168) */
  api(TAB === 'overview' || TAB === 'logs' ? 'status' : 'status&logs=0').then(function (data) {
    if (mine !== TICK_SEQ) return;
    /* ...and what was known of them kept for the pages that ask about them
       otherwise (the settings, the find box). */
    if (data && data.logs_left_out && STATUS && STATUS.logs) data.logs = STATUS.logs;
    TICK_BUSY = false;
    LAST_OK = Date.now();
    $('offline').hidden = true;
    /* The firewall's lines have a serial of their own (1.10.184): they move
       it every second, and only the Traffic page - and the front page, at
       most every half minute - is about them. */
    var fwMoved = data.serial_fw !== undefined && data.serial_fw !== SERIAL_FW;
    var fwReload = fwMoved && (TAB === 'traffic'
                               || (TAB === 'overview' && Date.now() - FW_MOVED_AT > 30000));
    if (fwReload) FW_MOVED_AT = Date.now();
    var moved = data.serial !== SERIAL || fwReload;
    SERIAL = data.serial;
    SERIAL_FW = data.serial_fw;
    STATUS = data;
    /* The php2shell notice, put away or brought back from the command line
       since the page was opened (1.10.220). */
    var phpBox = $('phpsafety');
    if (phpBox && phpBox.dataset.wanted) {
      phpBox.hidden = !!(data.php_notice && data.php_notice.hidden);
    }
    paintHeader(data, !moved);
    /* A change waiting to be kept, on every page (1.10.228). */
    if (typeof drawTrials === 'function') drawTrials(data);
    /* Whatever the database is busy with, wherever somebody is watching
       from. It rides on the status because the status is the one thing
       this page asks for every few seconds whatever tab is open, so a job
       somebody started and then walked away from is still there when they
       come back - and is there for the person on the other machine too. */
    /* On the file, a dump IS a copy of the file, and is counted as one: so
       while the dump button down there is waiting, a copy is drawn there
       too rather than in the card above it. */
    drawGoing('db-going', data.going, 'db-', 'db-dump');
    if (!drawGoing('db-dump-going', data.going, 'db-dump') && DUMP_ASKED) {
      drawGoing('db-dump-going', data.going, 'db-copy');
      if ($('db-going')) $('db-going').hidden = true;
    }
    drawDirectoryAlarm(data.directory_alarm);
    /* Who the domain's own controllers are, on every page rather than on the
       one that asked: the mark over a name or an address is wanted in the
       Failed attempts table and in a popup as much as on the Domain page. */
    dcLearn(data.controllers);
    dbBusyButtons(!!(data.going && Object.keys(data.going).some(function (k) {
      return k.indexOf('db-') === 0;
    })));
    /* Things that follow a setting rather than a page: switched off in
       another window, the button that makes an access list out of the bans
       should go away here too. */
    sayIfTheAclIsOffered();
    /* Nobody looking: the header is kept up to date and the list is not -
       it is drawn again the moment the tab is looked at. (1.10.168) */
    if (moved && document.hidden) {
      RELOAD_WHEN_SEEN = true;
      moved = false;
    } else if (moved && userHolds($('tab-' + TAB))) {
      /* Somebody is in the middle of something in the list - a key in a
         box drawn inside it, a button pressed with the keyboard, words
         being selected: drawing it again under them took the focus, the
         half-typed words and the selection away. It waits for them to
         finish, and is drawn the moment they have. (1.10.168) */
      RELOAD_WHEN_SEEN = true;
      moved = false;
    } else if (RELOAD_WHEN_SEEN && !document.hidden && !userHolds($('tab-' + TAB))) {
      RELOAD_WHEN_SEEN = false;
      moved = true;
    }
    if (moved) {
      /* The page's own refresh, not somebody asking: a search still going,
         or a slow one asked a moment ago, is left to finish (see `api`). */
      QUIET_TICK = true;
      try {
        if (TAB === 'overview') loadDashboard();
        /* The history again only when the bans have changed, or once a
           minute: it is a count of every ban there has been, and it was
           asked for on every record read. (1.10.175) */
        if (TAB === 'bans') {
          loadBans();
          if (Date.now() - PAST_AT > 60000 || (BANS && BANS.stamp !== PAST_STAMP)) loadPast();
        }
        if (TAB === 'failed') loadFailed();
        if (TAB === 'logins') {
          loadPassed();
          /* The access lists change when somebody edits one on ISE, not
             with every record read: a minute is soon enough. */
          if (PKIND === 'vpn' && Date.now() - ACLS_AT > 60000) {
            ACLS_AT = Date.now();
            loadAcls();
          }
        }
        if (TAB === 'events') loadEvents();
        if (TAB === 'traffic') loadTraffic(true);
        /* ...and the Domain page, which was never drawn again by itself: a
           lockout written down was seen only after a reload or a change of
           tab, though the daemon said at once that something had moved.
           Half a minute apart at most - the answers behind it are counted
           ahead and kept that long anyway. (1.10.178) */
        if (TAB === 'windows' && Date.now() - WIN_TICK_AT > 30000) {
          WIN_TICK_AT = Date.now();
          if (WPAGE_SHOWN === 'events') loadWinEvents(); else loadWindows();
        }
      } finally {
        QUIET_TICK = false;
      }
    }
  }).catch(function (e) {
    if (mine !== TICK_SEQ) return;
    TICK_BUSY = false;
    if (Date.now() - LAST_OK > 12000) {
      $('offline').hidden = false;
      $('offline').textContent = 'The daemon is not answering: ' + (e.message || e)
        + ' — what is on this page is as it was ' + ago(LAST_OK / 1000) + '.';
    }
  });
}

function startLive() {
  clearInterval(TIMER);
  TIMER = setInterval(tick, 5000);
  tick();
}
document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', function () {
  if (!document.hidden && LIVE) { TICK_HIDDEN = 0; tick(); }
  /* The Domain page's own asking stops while hidden; it starts again when
     the window is looked at again, or a re-read's bar stayed frozen for good
     after one glance at another tab (1.10.176). */
  if (!document.hidden && TAB === 'windows' && typeof loadWindows === 'function'
      && (WIN_GOING || Date.now() < WIN_WATCH_UNTIL || (WIN && WIN.working))) {
    loadWindows();
  }
});

$('updated').onclick = function () {
  LIVE = !LIVE;
  $('updated').classList.toggle('paused', !LIVE);
  $('updated').querySelector('b').textContent = LIVE ? 'live' : 'paused';
  if (LIVE) startLive(); else clearInterval(TIMER);
};

/* ---- the header ----------------------------------------------------------- */
/** How fast the logs are read: events a second now, over a minute and over
    five, and the database's share of it - small, in the corner, and only
    coloured when the daemon is not keeping up. Out of the status the header
    asks for anyway, so it costs nothing to show. */
function paintPace(p) {
  var host = $('pace');
  if (!host) return;
  if (!p) { host.hidden = true; return; }
  host.hidden = false;
  host.className = 'pace' + (p.level && p.level !== 'ok' ? ' ' + p.level : '');
  function rate(n) {
    n = Number(n || 0);
    return n >= 100 ? num(Math.round(n)) : num(Math.round(n * 10) / 10);
  }
  $('pace-words').textContent = 'EPS ' + rate(p.now) + ' \u00b7 1 min ' + rate(p.m1)
    + ' \u00b7 5 min ' + rate(p.m5) + (p.db_ms ? ' \u00b7 DB ' + rate(p.db_ms) + ' ms' : '');
  /* The words in a piece of their own, so that they are translated: inside
     the numbers they never were. (1.10.168) */
  if (p.level === 'over' || p.level === 'busy') {
    $('pace-words').appendChild(document.createTextNode(' \u00b7 '));
    $('pace-words').appendChild(el('span', '', p.level === 'over' ? 'not keeping up'
                                                                  : 'busy'));
  }
  explain(host, '<b>Events a second</b> the daemon is reading out of the logs: now (the last '
    + 'half a minute), over the last minute and over the last five.\n'
    + (p.db_qps !== undefined ? 'The database, the last minute: ' + rate(p.db_qps)
      + ' statement(s) a second, ' + rate(p.db_ms) + ' ms each on average, '
      + Math.round(100 * (p.db_share || 0)) + '% of the time spent waiting on it.\n' : '')
    + ((p.db_top || []).length ? (p.db_top || []).slice(0, 4).map(function (r) {
        return '  ' + Math.round(100 * (r.share || 0)) + '% ' + r.kind + ' \u2014 '
          + rate(r.per_s) + '/s, ' + rate(r.ms) + ' ms';
      }).join('\n') + '\n' : '')
    + 'Still to read: ' + bytes(p.behind || 0) + ' \u00b7 a pass over the logs takes '
    + (p.pass_s >= 1 ? span(p.pass_s) : 'under a second') + '.'
    + (p.says ? '\n<b>' + (p.level === 'over' ? 'Not keeping up' : 'Busy') + ':</b> ' + p.says
      : ''));
}

/** Which folders of a firewall's files are open on the Logs page. */
var LOGS_OPEN = {};
var LOG_GROUP_AT = 4;

function logTroubled(l) {
  return !!(l.error || l.says || !l.there || l.ahead);
}

/** The status's files as the Logs page lists them: see `log_rows_grouped`
    in the daemon, which this says the same as. */
function logRowsGrouped(logs, now, quietAfter) {
  var folders = {};
  var folderOf = function (l) { return String(l.path || '').replace(/\/[^\/]*$/, ''); };
  logs.forEach(function (l) {
    if (l.source !== 'fwlog' || logTroubled(l)) return;
    (folders[folderOf(l)] = folders[folderOf(l)] || []).push(l);
  });
  var out = [], done = {};
  logs.forEach(function (l) {
    var folder = folderOf(l);
    var group = l.source === 'fwlog' && !logTroubled(l) ? folders[folder] : null;
    if (!group || group.length < LOG_GROUP_AT) { out.push(l); return; }
    if (done[folder]) return;
    done[folder] = true;
    var one = {path: folder + '/', folder: folder, files: group.length, members: group,
               kind: group[0].kind, source: 'fwlog', there: true, at: 0, behind: 0,
               records: 0, quiet: 0, day: null};
    group.forEach(function (m) {
      one.at = Math.max(one.at, Number(m.at || 0));
      one.behind += Number(m.behind || 0);
      one.records += Number(m.records || 0);
      if (!m.at || Number(m.at) < now - quietAfter) one.quiet += 1;
    });
    out.push(one);
  });
  return out;
}

/** One folder of a firewall's files, as one row that opens to show them. */
function logFolderRow(l) {
  var open = !!LOGS_OPEN[l.folder];
  var tr = el('tr', 'log-folder');
  var td = el('td');
  var arrow = el('span', 'log-fold');
  arrow.innerHTML = iconMarkup(open ? 'unfold' : 'next', 12);
  td.appendChild(arrow);
  td.appendChild(el('span', 'mono small', l.path));
  td.appendChild(el('span', 'small muted', ' \u00b7 ' + num(l.files) + ' files'));
  if (l.quiet) td.appendChild(el('span', 'small muted', ', ' + num(l.quiet) + ' quiet'));
  tr.appendChild(td);
  var kindTd = el('td');
  var what = logHolds(l);
  var mark = el('span', 'tag ' + what.tone, what.words);
  explain(mark, what.says);
  kindTd.appendChild(mark);
  tr.appendChild(kindTd);
  tr.appendChild(el('td', 'small', LOG_HOLDS_WORDS[l.kind] || LOG_HOLDS_WORDS[l.source]
                                   || what.words));
  tr.appendChild(el('td', 'num', num(l.records)));
  tr.appendChild(el('td', 'num muted', '\u2013'));
  tr.appendChild(el('td', '', l.at ? ago(l.at) : 'not yet'));
  if (l.at) tr.appendChild(stampCell(l.at));
  else tr.appendChild(el('td', 'muted', 'nothing new yet'));
  tr.appendChild(el('td', l.behind ? '' : 'muted', l.behind ? bytes(l.behind) : 'up to date'));
  tr.appendChild(el('td', 'num' + (l.behind ? '' : ' muted'),
                    l.behind ? bytes(l.behind) : '\u2013'));
  tr.appendChild(el('td', 'muted', ''));
  opensWith(tr, 'The firewall writes a file for each kind of message into this folder, and '
    + 'every one of them is read; they are one row here, added up. A file with something '
    + 'wrong with it has a row of its own. <b>Quiet</b> ones have had nothing new for as '
    + 'long as the firewall\u2019s lines are kept \u2014 the file is still there because the '
    + 'rotation makes each one again, empty. Press to show or hide every file.', function () {
      if (LOGS_OPEN[l.folder]) delete LOGS_OPEN[l.folder]; else LOGS_OPEN[l.folder] = 1;
      if (STATUS) paintHeader(STATUS);
    });
  return tr;
}

/** One file of the Logs page. */
function logRow(l, inFolder) {
  var tr = el('tr', inFolder ? 'sub log-member' : '');
  var td = el('td');
  td.appendChild(el('span', 'mono small', l.path));
  tr.appendChild(td);
  var kindTd = el('td');
  /* What is IN the file, not how the file is doing. "failed" in a column
     beside one called TROUBLE reads as a verdict on the file itself. */
  var what = logHolds(l);
  var mark = el('span', 'tag ' + what.tone, what.words);
  explain(mark, what.says);
  kindTd.appendChild(mark);
  tr.appendChild(kindTd);
  /* ...and in words, the way `isemon --status` says it. */
  var holdsWords = LOG_HOLDS_WORDS[l.kind] || LOG_HOLDS_WORDS[l.source] || what.words;
  var holdsTd = el('td', 'small', holdsWords);
  if (what.whole) explain(holdsTd, what.whole.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + what.whole.slice(1)
                          + '.');
  tr.appendChild(holdsTd);
  tr.appendChild(el('td', 'num', num(l.records)));
  /* The machine's own last day, beside the file: a node of a pair at
     nought next to one carrying the work is a failover, both at nought is
     a node that stopped sending. One column scales to any number of logs,
     where the same numbers strung along the heading did not. */
  tr.appendChild(el('td', 'num' + (l.day ? '' : ' muted'),
                    l.day === null || l.day === undefined ? '–' : num(l.day)));
  tr.appendChild(el('td', '', l.there ? (l.at ? ago(l.at) : 'not yet') : '–'));
  if (l.at) tr.appendChild(stampCell(l.at));
  else tr.appendChild(el('td', 'muted', 'nothing new yet'));
  var last = el('td');
  if (!l.there) {
    var gone = el('span', 'tag warn', 'not there');
    gone.title = 'This file does not exist. On a pair where one node has been quiet, that is '
      + 'ordinary — ISE writes the log when it has something to write. It only matters if '
      + 'no node has one.';
    last.appendChild(gone);
  }
  else if (l.error) {
    /* What went wrong, in words somebody can act on, with the raw message
       behind it. "[Errno 13] Permission denied" is the truth and is no
       use: what a person needs to know is that this daemon is not root. */
    var bad = el('span', 'tag warn', l.says && l.says.indexOf('may not read') >= 0
                 ? 'it may not read it' : (l.says || l.error));
    explain(bad, (l.says && l.says !== l.error ? l.says + '. ' : '')
            + (l.fix ? '\nOne command puts it right:\n<code>' + l.fix + '</code>\n' : '')
            + '\nThe message itself: <code>' + l.error + '</code>');
    last.appendChild(bad);
  }
  else if (l.ahead) {
    if (l.behind) {
      last.appendChild(el('span', 'small muted', bytes(l.behind) + ' behind · '));
    }
    /* The file reads perfectly and every record in it is hours from where
       it belongs. Nothing else on this page can say so: the rows look
       right, the counts look right, and the only thing that is wrong is
       which second they are filed under. */
    var early = el('span', 'tag warn', 'dated ' + span(l.ahead) + ' ahead');
    explain(early, l.ahead_says || '');
    last.appendChild(early);
  }
  /* How much is in the column beside: this one says what that means, rather
     than the same number twice. (1.10.168) */
  else last.appendChild(el('span', l.behind ? '' : 'muted',
                           l.behind ? 'catching up' : 'up to date'));
  tr.appendChild(last);
  tr.appendChild(el('td', 'num' + (l.behind ? '' : ' muted'),
                    l.behind ? bytes(l.behind) : '\u2013'));
  var trouble = l.says || l.error || (l.there ? '' : 'the file is not there')
    || (l.ahead ? 'dated ' + span(l.ahead) + ' ahead of the clock' : '');
  tr.appendChild(el('td', trouble ? 'small t-trouble' : 'muted', trouble || ''));
  opensWith(tr, 'Where this file has been read up to, and what that means.',
            function () { peekLog(l); });
  return tr;
}

function paintHeader(s, quiet) {
  paintPace(s && s.pace);
  /* The names that never existed come with every poll of the header, so a
     list drawn on any page can mark them without having been on the page
     that happens to load them. */
  if (s && s.bogus_names) BOGUS = s.bogus_names;
  var pill = $('state-pill');
  var dot = pill.querySelector('.dot'), words = pill.querySelector('span:last-child');
  var kind = 'ok', said = 'watching';
  /* One verdict, worked out by the daemon out of everything it knows - see
     `health` there. It used to be worked out here, out of four of the twenty
     facts on this page, and so it said "watching", in green, while the
     database was refusing every write, while a log was there but unreadable,
     while no ISE log was configured at all, and while every record arriving
     was too old to be judged. The rules live in one place now. */
  var health = s.health || {};
  if (health.level === 'crit' || health.level === 'warn') {
    kind = health.level;
    said = health.says || kind;
  } else if (s.blocked.now) { said = s.blocked.now + ' kept out'; }
  pill.className = 'pill ' + kind;
  words.textContent = said;
  pill.title = s.blacklist_on
    ? s.trigger + ' earns a ban · ' + s.lengths
    : 'Nothing is being enforced and nothing of ours is on the router. The list is kept as it is.';
  /* The one clock on the page that is not a record of anything: it says the
     page is still being answered, so it stays the time alone. */
  $('updated-at').textContent = clockWords(new Date(s.now * 1000));
  $('updated-at').title = 'the daemon answered at ' + stamp(s.now);

  var bans = $('tabn-bans');
  bans.textContent = s.blocked.now || '';
  bans.className = 'count' + (s.blocked.now ? ' hot' : '');
  $('tabn-failed').textContent = s.today.failed ? num(s.today.failed) : '';

  /* Both cards count every door, as they always have. What is new is that
     they say how much of it was the VPN wherever the two differ: on a site
     that reads its eduroam as well, twelve thousand passed is other
     institutions' visitors getting on to the wireless and says nothing
     whatever about the VPN, which is the only door any of this judges. */
  var vpnShare = function (whole, vpn) {
    return (vpn === undefined || vpn === null || vpn === whole) ? ''
      : ' \u00b7 ' + num(vpn) + ' of them the VPN';
  };
  $('m-failed').textContent = s.today.counting ? '\u2026' : num(s.today.failed);
  /* Each may be still being worked out after a start (1.10.184). */
  var tf = (s.totals || {}).failed || {}, tp = (s.totals || {}).passed || {};
  $('m-failed-sub').textContent = num(tf.rows) + ' ever, from '
    + num(tf.addresses) + ' address(es)'
    + vpnShare(s.today.failed, s.today.failed_vpn);
  $('m-passed').textContent = s.today.counting ? '\u2026' : num(s.today.passed);
  $('m-passed-sub').textContent = num(tp.rows) + ' ever'
    + vpnShare(s.today.passed, s.today.passed_vpn);
  $('m-blocked').textContent = num(s.blocked.now);
  $('m-blocked-sub').textContent = s.blocked.forever + ' for good · '
    + s.blocked.temporary + ' on a clock';
  var r = s.mikrotik;
  $('m-router').textContent = !r.ready ? '–' : (r.trouble ? 'out of sync' : 'in sync');
  /* Only the colour changes here. Writing the whole class list, which is what
     this used to do, threw away `tappable` every few seconds and left the
     card looking pressable and not being it. */
  var routerCard = $('m-router').parentNode;
  ['ok', 'warn', 'crit', 'info'].forEach(function (one) { routerCard.classList.remove(one); });
  routerCard.classList.add(!r.ready ? 'info' : (r.trouble ? 'crit' : 'ok'));
  $('m-router-sub').textContent = !r.ready ? 'no firewall is set up'
    : (r.trouble ? r.trouble.slice(0, 70)
       : (r.host + ' · ' + r.list + (r.at ? ' · written ' + ago(r.at) : ' · never written')));

  /* The token half of the legend only exists when the token logs are being
     read: a colour in a legend that never appears in the chart is a question
     nobody can answer. */
  ['leg-otp-bad', 'leg-otp-good'].forEach(function (id) {
    var node = $(id);
    if (node) node.hidden = !s.otp_on;
  });
  /* ...and ISE's own six, which a site reading no ISE has no use for: a
     colour in a legend that never appears in the chart is a question nobody
     can answer. Kept while anything of ISE's is in the window, which is the
     chart's own business and not this line's. */
  Array.prototype.forEach.call(document.querySelectorAll('.legend .leg-ise'),
    function (node) { node.hidden = s.ise_on === false; });
  ['leg-tac-bad', 'leg-tac-good'].forEach(function (id) {
    var node = $(id);
    if (node) node.hidden = !s.tacplus_on;
