  {kind: 'tacplus', words: 'TAC_Plus', icon: 'terminal',
   says: 'Somebody signing in to a switch or a router through a TACACS+ daemon of this '
     + 'site\u2019s own \u2014 tac_plus rather than ISE \u2014 and every command they ran '
     + 'once they were in. A different system from the TACACS+ beside it, and which of '
     + 'the two answered is most of what somebody wants to know. Nothing here is ever '
     + 'judged: signing in to a switch never has been, through ISE either.'},
  {kind: 'dot1x-lan', words: '802.1X LAN', icon: 'ethernet',
   says: 'A machine on a socket, authenticating itself with 802.1X \u2014 a certificate or a '
     + 'domain account, over the wire.'},
  {kind: 'dot1x-wifi', words: '802.1X Wi-Fi', icon: 'wifi',
   says: 'A machine or a person on the wireless, authenticating with 802.1X.'},
  {kind: 'mab-lan', words: 'MAB LAN', icon: 'plug',
   says: 'MAC Authentication Bypass on a socket: something with no supplicant at all \u2014 a '
     + 'printer, a camera, a door controller \u2014 let in, or not, by its MAC address alone.'},
  {kind: 'mab-wifi', words: 'MAB Wi-Fi', icon: 'plug',
   says: 'MAC Authentication Bypass on the wireless: an endpoint let in by its MAC address '
     + 'rather than by anything it knows.'},
  /* The same doors again, answered by a Windows NPS rather than by ISE. Two
     systems, kept apart the way ISE's TACACS+ and tac_plus are: which of them
     let a machine on is most of what somebody wants to know. Each is drawn
     only where NPS has really answered through it - a site with no VPN
